      ],
      "lineAnnotations": {
        "13": {"synopsis":"A table of decimal products for the digit-by-digit multiplication in the SYS high chip\n($F56C-$F5B8). Row a (1-9), column b (0-9) holds a x b as two BCD digits, at offset $0003 +\n10a + b: the code forms L = 10a + b + 3 with H = $C0 ($F577-$F599) and adds the byte to the\npartial product with DAA. Row 0 would be the ten zeros at $0003-$000C, but the code skips a\nzero digit ($F575) and never reads it."},
        "103": {"synopsis":"The statement dispatch table: one word per token, at $0067 + 2 x ($FF - token). This chip's\n$1B92-$1BA3 reads it for tokens $FD (\":\") down to $CA (it complements the token, doubles it\nand adds $67, with H = $C0); a token from $FE up ends the statement first ($1B89), so the\nwords for $FF and $FE ($0067, $0069) are never read, and one below $CA goes to the SYS high\nchip's $E3BC. So $006B is \":\", $006D REM, $006F DATA, ... $00C5 LPRINT ($D0). The words for\ntokens $CF-$CA ($00C7-$00D1) are the first six of the calculator's operation table at\nCALC_OP_TABLE; those tokens are named \"!\" in the token table and so never typed."},
        "199": {"synopsis":"The calculator's operation table: 15 words, operation n (0-14) at $00C7 + 2n. RST $18 (RAM\n$0018, a jump to the SYS high chip's $FB82) interprets the bytes after it as operations:\nbits 6-0 the operation, bit 7 set on the last one ($FB91-$FB9B forms L = 2n + $C7 with H =\n$C0). Operation 5 (the word at $00D1, $EA82) loads the constant whose index is the next byte\nfrom NUM_CONSTANTS. Operations 0-5 are also the statement words of the unused tokens\n$CF-$CA."},
        "229": {"synopsis":"The copyright text, ended by $00. Its last byte, $0111, is also the first byte of constant 0\n(see NUM_CONSTANTS)."},
        "274": {"synopsis":"Floating-point constants for the calculator, 7 bytes each: six BCD bytes least significant\nfirst (12 digits, the first digit before the decimal point), then the exponent, $40 for\n10^0, bit 7 the sign. Constant n is at $0111 + 7n: the SYS high chip's $EA82 (calculator\noperation 5) takes n from the operation stream and forms 7n + $C111 ($EA8A-$EA97), then\n$EAA2 copies the number to the calculator stack. Constant 0 starts at $0111, the copyright\ntext's $00. Constants 0 and 1 are 0.5 and 1, used by several of the routines below."},
        "287": {"synopsis":"Constants 2-8 for the routine at the SYS high chip's $EAF1 (its RST $18 streams at\n$EB14-$EB3C): 2 - sqrt(3) (0.267949192431), sqrt(3), sqrt(3) - 1 (0.732050807569), then four\ncoefficients (-1.44008344874, -0.720026848898, 4.32025038919, 4.75222584599) evaluated as a\nchain in the stream at $EB3C. The routine then adds pi/6, pi/2 or pi/3 ($EB5F-$EB71), so it\nis ATN (observed: PRINT ATN(1) runs it; LOG, EXP, SQR, SIN and TAN do not)."},
        "336": {"synopsis":"Constants 9-16 for the RST $18 streams at the SYS high chip's $EBB8 and $EBED:\n0.868588963807 (2 / ln 10), 1.151 (ln 10 / 2 cut to four digits), then six coefficients\n(2.92546497023E-4, 504.464889506, 14.0082997563, 0.0332873646516, 1008.92977901,\n112.094081097) used in two chains in the stream at $EBED, the EXP function's (observed:\nPRINT EXP(1) runs both streams; LOG, ATN, SQR, SIN and TAN do not)."},
        "392": {"synopsis":"Constants 17-24 for the RST $18 streams at the SYS high chip's $ED49 and $ED85: ln 10\n(2.30258509299), six coefficients (-2.91568143790, 3.16303491557, -0.673581601478,\n-10.0704069542, 16.9669814021, -8.19080045467) in two chains, and 2 / ln 10 again (constant\n24 repeats constant 9)."},
        "448": {"synopsis":"Constants 25-29 for the RST $18 stream at the SYS high chip's $EE9A: close to -1/3!, 1/5!,\n-1/7!, 1/9! and -1/11! (-0.166666666088 ... -2.38683464060E-8), evaluated as a chain: the\nterms of a sine series."},
        "483": {"synopsis":"Constants 30-32: 0.223607 and 0.894427 (sqrt(5) / 10 and 2 / sqrt(5) to six digits), loaded\nby the RST $18 stream at the SYS high chip's $EEE2, and 0.316227766017 (sqrt(0.1)), loaded\nby the streams at $EC23, $ED38 and $EF03."},
        "504": {"synopsis":"Constants 33-37, angles: 57.2957795131 (180 / pi; no reader found in the SYS high chip), pi\n(streams at $EDE3, $EE5E, $EE83), pi/2 (streams at $EBA7, $EE6F, $F001), pi/3 and pi/6. The\nSYS high chip's $EB5F-$EB6B loads pi/6, pi/2 or pi/3 by address ($C214, $C206, $C20D) and\npasses it to $EAA2."},
        "539": {"synopsis":"Constants 38 and 39: 32768 (streams at the SYS high chip's $E0EA and $EDF8) and\n9.999999999E62, the largest number the format holds (the stream at $EC85)."},
        "553": {"synopsis":"The reset code, reached by the JP at offset 0, which runs at $0000 because port 02h is 0\nafter a reset and page 0 shows SYS. This part runs at $0229: interrupts off, mode 1, SYS\npaged into pages 0 and 3 (port 02h = $40), then on at RESET_TO_EXTH ($0233) in the same\npage-0 copy.","comment":"Runs at $0229 after a reset"},
        "556": {"comment":"SYS in pages 0 and 3"},
        "560": {"comment":"Stays in the page-0 copy"},
        "563": {"synopsis":"Runs at $0233. Pages EXT into page 3 (port 02h = $C0) and jumps to EXTH's $F13D, which\ncompares the RAM copies at $0B23 with its own, sets $0B21 from the result and comes back to\nRESET_RESUME ($023A) with port 02h restored.","comment":"EXT (card window + EXTH) in page 3"},
        "567": {"comment":"EXTH's $F13D checks RAM, returns to RESET_RESUME ($023A)"},
        "570": {"synopsis":"Runs at $023A, where EXTH's $F163 jumps back. Pages SYS into page 3 again and continues in\nthat copy at RESET_HW_INIT ($C241).","comment":"SYS in pages 0 and 3"},
        "574": {"comment":"On in the page-3 copy"},
        "577": {"synopsis":"Runs at $C241. Pages U0 into page 0 and video RAM into page 2 (port 02h = $50), clears the\nfour palette registers (ports 60h-63h), writes the 16 CRTC registers from CRTC_TABLE (R15\nfirst), sets port 06h to $80, then decides between a cold start (the memory test, at $026F)\nand a warm one (WARM_DELAY): $0B22 holding $FF forces a cold start and clears $0B21;\notherwise $0B21 as EXTH's $F13D left it decides, $FF meaning warm.","comment":"U0 in page 0, video RAM in page 2, SYS in page 3"},
        "581": {"comment":"Palette value 0"},
        "582": {"comment":"4 registers from port 60h"},
        "585": {"synopsis":"Clears the palette registers, ports 60h-63h."},
        "590": {"comment":"From R15 down to R0"},
        "595": {"synopsis":"Writes the 16 CRTC registers: register B - 1 gets the next byte of CRTC_TABLE.","comment":"Register number: 15 down to 0"},
        "599": {"comment":"Its value"},
        "605": {"comment":"Port 06h bit 7 set, video mode 0"},
        "609": {"comment":"$0B22 = $FF forces a cold start"},
        "615": {"comment":"Clear $0B21: no warm start"},
        "618": {"comment":"Skipped into at $026B: this makes A nonzero"},
        "620": {"comment":"$0B21 held $FF: a warm start?"},
        "623": {"comment":"Cold start: the stack in U2 for now"},
        "626": {"comment":"Test U0, $0000-$3FFF"},
        "632": {"comment":"Passed?"},
        "634": {"synopsis":"U0 failed the memory test: cycles the border colour for ever, as there is no RAM for a\nstack.","comment":"Next border colour, for ever"},
        "639": {"synopsis":"Moves the stack into the tested U0 at $16AC and tests video RAM at $8000-$BFFF (page 2); on\na failure writes $88 to the port in C (the low byte of the count the test left) and carries\non.","comment":"The stack in tested U0"},
        "642": {"comment":"Video RAM, paged at $8000"},
        "650": {"comment":"Video RAM failed"},
        "654": {"synopsis":"Pages U2 into page 2 (port 02h = $70) and tests U1 and, if it passes, U2; HL - 1 is then the\nlast good address, kept in $0B19 as the top of RAM ($3FFF, $7FFF or $BFFF, or below a\nfailing byte). Then SYS back into page 0, the stack at $BFFF, and on at TEST_U3 ($02A9) in\nthe page-0 copy.","comment":"U0 in page 0, U2 in page 2, SYS in page 3"},
        "658": {"comment":"Test U1, $4000-$7FFF"},
        "664": {"comment":"And U2 if U1 passed"},
        "667": {"comment":"The last good address"},
        "668": {"comment":"$0B19: the top of RAM"},
        "671": {"comment":"SYS in pages 0 and 3"},
        "678": {"comment":"Into the page-0 copy before page 3 changes"},
        "681": {"synopsis":"Runs at $02A9. Pages U3 into page 3 (port 02h = $80) and tests it through the page-0 copy of\nthe test (MEMTEST_LOW); A' keeps the result for EXTH, $00 passed or $FF failed.","comment":"U3 in page 3, SYS in page 0"},
        "685": {"comment":"Test U3, $C000-$FFFF"},
        "691": {"comment":"$00: U3 passed"},
        "695": {"comment":"$FF: U3 failed"},
        "696": {"synopsis":"Keeps the U3 result in A' and joins the common path.","comment":"A' = the U3 result, for EXTH's $F000"},
        "699": {"synopsis":"A warm start skips the memory test and waits instead: HL counts 65536 down from 0 (A is 0\nhere).","comment":"HL = 0: 65536 loops"},
        "701": {"synopsis":"Counts HL down to 0."},
        "706": {"synopsis":"Pages SYS into page 3 and goes on at RESET_EXTH_INIT ($02C9) in the page-0 copy.","comment":"SYS in pages 0 and 3"},
        "710": {"comment":"Into the page-0 copy before page 3 changes"},
        "713": {"synopsis":"Runs at $02C9. Pages EXT into page 3 and jumps to EXTH's $F000, which sets up RAM (the RAM\ntables at $0B00-$0B0F, the restart vectors at $0030-$003F, the code at $0B23, $0B1B from A'\nafter a cold start) and comes back to BOOT_IO_INIT through its $FFF0 with port 02h = $70.","comment":"EXT in page 3"},
        "717": {"comment":"The stack at $16AC"},
        "720": {"comment":"EXTH's $F000 sets up RAM, returns to BOOT_IO_INIT ($C2D3)"},
        "723": {"synopsis":"Runs at $C2D3, where EXTH's $F000 returns. Writes 0 to the I/O ports (03h, 07h, 58h-5Bh, the\nborder 00h) and to their RAM copies $0B11 (port 03h) and $0B4F (the border), sets port 06h\nand its copy $0B13 to $80, then calls the four routines of BOOT_INIT_TABLE (each with C =\n$70), restoring port 02h to $70 after each; then on at CART_CHECK ($030D) in the page-0\ncopy.","comment":"0 for the ports below"},
        "724": {"comment":"$0B11: the copy of port 03h"},
        "727": {"comment":"Keyboard row 0, card 0 in the EXT window"},
        "729": {"comment":"Clear the interrupt"},
        "739": {"comment":"Border colour 0"},
        "741": {"comment":"$0B4F: the border colour"},
        "744": {"comment":"Port 06h bit 7 set"},
        "748": {"comment":"$0B13: the copy of port 06h"},
        "751": {"comment":"Four routines to call"},
        "754": {"comment":"B = 4 routines, C = $70"},
        "757": {"synopsis":"Calls the next routine of BOOT_INIT_TABLE and restores port 02h to C ($70)."},
        "758": {"comment":"DE = the next routine"},
        "763": {"comment":"Call it"},
        "769": {"comment":"Restore the paging: U0, U2, SYS"},
        "774": {"comment":"SYS in pages 0 and 3, U2 in page 2"},
        "778": {"comment":"Into the page-0 copy before page 3 changes"},
        "781": {"synopsis":"Runs at $030D. Pages the cartridge into page 3 (port 02h = $20) and compares its first four\nbytes with CART_SIGNATURE (\"MOPS\"); when they match, jumps to the cartridge at $C004.","comment":"The cartridge in page 3, U2 in page 2"},
        "785": {"comment":"The cartridge's first bytes"},
        "788": {"comment":"\"MOPS\", read through page 0"},
        "793": {"synopsis":"Compares the next signature byte."},
        "794": {"comment":"Same as the cartridge's?"},
        "801": {"synopsis":"Jumps to HL: called as a routine, it calls the routine at HL. The cartridge check falls into\nit with HL = $C004, starting the cartridge."},
        "802": {"synopsis":"No cartridge signature: pages SYS into page 3 and continues in that copy at BOOT_TO_BASIC\n($C329).","comment":"SYS in pages 0 and 3, U2 in page 2"},
        "806": {"comment":"On in the page-3 copy"},
        "809": {"synopsis":"Runs at $C329. Pages U0 into page 0 (port 02h = $70, recorded in $0003), enables interrupts\nand starts BASIC at BASIC_START ($D9EF).","comment":"U0, U2 in pages 0-2, SYS in page 3"},
        "813": {"comment":"$0003: the current paging value"},
        "817": {"comment":"Into BASIC's start"},
        "820": {"synopsis":"\"MOPS\": the four bytes CART_CHECK looks for at the start of a cartridge."},
        "824": {"synopsis":"The memory test for code running in page 0 (called at $0338 by TEST_U3): the same as\nMEMTEST, but calls the page-0 copy of the fill and check.","comment":"Runs at $0338: keep the block start"},
        "825": {"comment":"$55 pass, in the page-0 copy"},
        "828": {"comment":"Then the $AA pass"},
        "830": {"synopsis":"Tests the 16K block at HL: fills it with $55 and checks it, then with $AA, clearing each\nbyte to 0 after its check.\nEntry: HL = the block start.\nExit: Z set if both passes held; NZ with HL at the first failing byte otherwise. On success\nHL = the block start + $4000.\nChanges: A, BC, DE."},
        "831": {"comment":"The $55 pass"},
        "834": {"synopsis":"After the $55 pass: on a failure returns NZ; otherwise runs the $AA pass from the block\nstart."},
        "835": {"comment":"A failure: HL at the bad byte"},
        "836": {"comment":"The block start again"},
        "837": {"comment":"Pattern $AA (skips the ld a,$55 at $0348)"},
        "839": {"comment":"Hides $0348: ld a,$55"},
        "842": {"comment":"Keep the block start"},
        "843": {"comment":"DE one byte ahead: the LDIR fills"},
        "847": {"comment":"$3FFF more bytes: 16K"},
        "852": {"comment":"Back to the start"},
        "853": {"comment":"BC = $4000 bytes to check"},
        "855": {"comment":"A = the pattern - 1"},
        "856": {"synopsis":"Checks one byte: decrements it, compares with the pattern - 1 (CPI moves on and counts\ndown), then clears it. NZ returns at the first mismatch with HL on it; PO (the count run\nout) returns Z.","comment":"Pattern - 1 if the byte held the pattern"},
        "857": {"comment":"Matches? HL on, BC down"},
        "859": {"comment":"Back to the byte"},
        "860": {"comment":"Clear it"},
        "862": {"comment":"Mismatch: NZ, HL on the bad byte"},
        "864": {"comment":"All $4000 checked: Z set"},
        "867": {"synopsis":"The system call, RST $30 followed by a code byte. RAM $0030 jumps to $0B23 (EXTH's copy),\nwhich fetches the code into A', saves the paging, pages SYS into page 3 and jumps here.\nSaves HL, IX, IY and the alternate registers, brings the code back into A and calls\nSYSCALL_DISPATCH; leaves through RAM $0B37, which restores the paging and returns.\nEntry: A' = the code; BC, DE the function's arguments.\nExit: A = 0 (Z) on success, an error code otherwise; HL, IX, IY and the alternate set\npreserved."},
        "872": {"comment":"Save the alternate set"},
        "877": {"comment":"A = the code"},
        "891": {"comment":"RAM $0B37: restore the paging, return"},
        "894": {"synopsis":"Dispatches a system call code.\n1. Bit 7 of the code picks the RAM table: set $0B00, clear $0B08; bits 6-4 an entry (0-7),\n   bits 3-0 the function. Entry 1 of $0B00 holding 2 is set back to 1 first.\n2. Entry 7 goes straight to device 7. For 0-6 the entry's byte is read: $FF (unused) is\n   allowed only for functions 3 and up; a byte of 7-$7F is an error ($FE); bit 7 set hands\n   the call to EXTH's $F166 (a card, slot = bits 1-0); otherwise functions 0-2 use the byte\n   as the device number, functions 3 and up the entry number itself.\n3. Device 6 goes to EXTH's $F16C (the card named in entry 7). Others look up DEVICE_TABLE: a\n   function past the device's count is an error ($FF); else the function is called with A =\n   the code and the caller's BC and DE.\n4. A nonzero result, outside the interrupt ($0B20 not $FF), goes to EXTH's $F1E6, which\n   resets the entry to its default and returns to SC_ERR_RETURN.\nEntry: A = the code.\nExit: A = the result, 0 for success.","comment":"Saved for the result check"},
        "897": {"comment":"C = the code"},
        "898": {"comment":"Sign flag = bit 7, kept in A'"},
        "900": {"comment":"Entry 1 of the $0B00 table"},
        "904": {"comment":"Holding 2?"},
        "908": {"comment":"Then 1"},
        "909": {"synopsis":"Picks the RAM table by bit 7 of the code: $0B00 when set, $0B08 when clear.","comment":"Bit 7 set: table $0B00"},
        "912": {"comment":"Bit 7 into the carry"},
        "916": {"comment":"Bit 7 clear: table $0B08"},
        "919": {"synopsis":"Splits the code: B = bits 6-4 (the entry), C = bits 3-0 (the function).","comment":"Bits 6-4, rotated up by one"},
        "924": {"comment":"B = the entry, 0-7"},
        "926": {"comment":"C = the function, 0-15"},
        "930": {"comment":"Entry 7: device 7, no lookup"},
        "934": {"comment":"HL = the entry in the RAM table"},
        "938": {"comment":"A = $FF"},
        "939": {"comment":"An unused entry?"},
        "943": {"comment":"Function 3 or above: entry = device"},
        "947": {"synopsis":"Checks the entry's byte: 7-$7F (bit 7 ignored) is an error, $FE.","comment":"The entry's byte, bit 7 aside"},
        "950": {"comment":"A device number 0-6?"},
        "954": {"comment":"No: error $FE"},
        "956": {"synopsis":"Fails the call with the error code in A: drops the saved DE and BC and goes to the result\ncheck."},
        "958": {"comment":"Report the error"},
        "960": {"synopsis":"An entry with bit 7 set names a card: the call goes to EXTH's $F166.","comment":"A card slot rather than a device?"},
        "964": {"comment":"EXTH's $F166: call the card"},
        "967": {"synopsis":"Jumps to HL in EXTH through the SYS high chip's $FFF0, which pages EXT into page 3 and\nrecords the paging in $0003.","comment":"The SYS high chip's $FFF0: EXTH at HL"},
        "970": {"synopsis":"Functions 0-2 take the device number from the entry; functions 3 and up keep the entry\nnumber.","comment":"Functions 0-2 follow the entry"},
        "975": {"comment":"B = the device in the entry"},
        "976": {"synopsis":"Device 6 is handled by EXTH's $F16C, which uses entry 7 of the RAM table as the card slot.","comment":"Device 6?"},
        "979": {"comment":"EXTH's $F16C: the card in entry 7"},
        "984": {"synopsis":"Calls function C of device B through DEVICE_TABLE; a function beyond the device's count\nfails with $FF. Also entered from the interrupt's INT_POLL_DEVS with a return address pushed\nin place of the saved registers.","comment":"The device table"},
        "987": {"comment":"HL = the device's word"},
        "992": {"comment":"HL = its function list"},
        "996": {"comment":"The number of functions"},
        "998": {"comment":"Function C beyond the list?"},
        "999": {"comment":"Error $FF: no such function"},
        "1003": {"comment":"DE = the first function word"},
        "1005": {"comment":"HL = the word of function C"},
        "1010": {"comment":"HL = the function"},
        "1014": {"comment":"A = the code again"},
        "1015": {"comment":"The caller's DE and BC"},
        "1017": {"comment":"Call the function"},
        "1020": {"synopsis":"Checks the function's result: 0 returns at once. An error inside the interrupt ($0B20 = $FF)\nis returned as it is; otherwise EXTH's $F1E6 is called with A = the code, resets the RAM\ntable entry to its default and comes back to SC_ERR_RETURN. EXTH's $F1D2 also returns here\nafter calling a card.","comment":"Success?"},
        "1021": {"comment":"H = the code saved at entry"},
        "1024": {"comment":"Inside the interrupt?"},
        "1032": {"comment":"A = the code"},
        "1033": {"comment":"EXTH's $F1E6: reset the entry, back to SC_ERR_RETURN"},
        "1038": {"synopsis":"Where EXTH's $F1E6 returns (through $FFF0) after resetting the RAM table entry: restores DE,\nBC and the error code, and returns."},
        "1040": {"synopsis":"Returns the error code in A."},
        "1042": {"synopsis":"The interrupt handler. RAM $0038 (EXTH's copy) saves AF, pages SYS into page 3 (port 02h =\n$70) and jumps here. Saves the paging value from $0003 and every register, calls\nINT_SERVICE, restores them and leaves through RAM $0B41, which writes the saved paging back\nto $0003 and port 02h, pops AF, enables interrupts and returns.","comment":"The paging before the interrupt"},
        "1053": {"comment":"And the alternate set"},
        "1059": {"comment":"Service the interrupt"},
        "1075": {"comment":"A = the saved paging value"},
        "1076": {"comment":"RAM $0B41: restore the paging, EI, return"},
        "1079": {"synopsis":"The interrupt's work.\n1. Sets $0B20 to $FF (inside the interrupt; system call errors are not reported), counts the\n   frame counter $0B1D-$0B1E up and writes the border colour $0B4F to port 00h.\n2. Reads the status port 59h and acknowledges on port 07h. If bit 4 was 0 (the shared\n   cursor/sound interrupt), INT_POLL_DEVS calls the devices and cards selected in $0B10.\n3. If any expansion slot signals an interrupt (bits 3-0 of port 59h, 0 = pending), writes\n   $00 or $80 (from bits 2-5 of $0B1F) to port 58h + slot for each such slot and has EXTH's\n   $F227 serve them, returning to INT_END.","comment":"$0B20 = $FF: inside the interrupt"},
        "1084": {"comment":"$0B1D: the frame counter"},
        "1091": {"comment":"$0B4F: the border colour"},
        "1096": {"comment":"Status: bits 3-0 slot interrupts, bit 4 ours"},
        "1099": {"comment":"The shared interrupt pending (0)?"},
        "1101": {"comment":"Mark it served in the copy"},
        "1103": {"comment":"Acknowledge it"},
        "1105": {"comment":"Poll the devices if it was pending"},
        "1108": {"comment":"Any slot interrupt (a 0 in bits 3-0)?"},
        "1112": {"comment":"None: done"},
        "1114": {"comment":"$0B1F bits 2-5: a value per slot"},
        "1119": {"comment":"H: slot bits to shift out"},
        "1120": {"comment":"L: the status, kept for EXTH"},
        "1121": {"comment":"4 slots, ports 58h-5Bh"},
        "1125": {"synopsis":"For each of the four slots: A = $80 if its $0B1F bit is set, else $00; written to port 58h +\nslot when the slot's status bit is 0."},
        "1126": {"comment":"The slot's $0B1F bit into bit 7"},
        "1129": {"comment":"Has this slot no interrupt?"},
        "1133": {"comment":"Write to its port 58h-5Bh"},
        "1135": {"synopsis":"Moves on to the next slot's port."},
        "1138": {"comment":"C = the status for EXTH"},
        "1139": {"comment":"Return to INT_END"},
        "1142": {"comment":"EXTH's $F227 serves the slots"},
        "1144": {"synopsis":"Clears $0B20: the interrupt is over.","comment":"$0B20 = 0: outside the interrupt"},
        "1149": {"synopsis":"Calls function 0 of each device 0-3 whose bit (0-3) in $0B10 is 0, through SC_CALL_DEVICE\nwith C = 0, restoring port 02h to $70 after each; then has EXTH's $F227 serve the cards\nwhose bits 4-7 in $0B10 are 0."},
        "1150": {"comment":"$0B10: bits 0-3 devices, 4-7 cards"},
        "1154": {"comment":"Devices 0-3"},
        "1156": {"synopsis":"Takes the next device's bit; a 0 calls the device's function 0.","comment":"The next device's bit"},
        "1159": {"comment":"Set: skip it"},
        "1161": {"comment":"B = the device, 0-3"},
        "1165": {"comment":"Function 0"},
        "1167": {"comment":"The return address, and three dummy words"},
        "1170": {"comment":"In place of the dispatcher's AF, BC, DE"},
        "1174": {"comment":"Call the device's function 0"},
        "1177": {"synopsis":"Restores the paging after a device and moves on to the next.","comment":"U0, U2, SYS again"},
        "1184": {"comment":"Return to $04AA"},
        "1187": {"synopsis":"Jumps to EXTH's $F227 with C = the slot bits (0 = serve) and the return address on the\nstack."},
        "1188": {"comment":"EXTH's $F227 serves the cards"},
        "1194": {"comment":"EXTH returns here"},
        "1196": {"synopsis":"Device 7's function list: the count (5), then the words for functions 0-4 (system call codes\n$70-$74)."},
        "1207": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $71: reserves DE bytes at the top of RAM by lowering $0B19, provided the new\ntop stays at $1EAC or above.\nEntry: DE = the number of bytes.\nExit: A = 0 and DE = the start of the reserved block (the new top + 1); A = $FB when there\nis not enough room.\nChanges: HL.","comment":"$0B19: the top of RAM"},
        "1210": {"comment":"Error $FB: not enough room"},
        "1213": {"comment":"The new top"},
        "1215": {"comment":"More than all of RAM?"},
        "1217": {"comment":"The lowest top allowed"},
        "1223": {"comment":"Below $1EAC?"},
        "1224": {"comment":"DE = the reserved block"},
        "1227": {"comment":"Lower the top of RAM"},
        "1232": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $72: finds the card with the given name and type (FIND_CARD) and stores its\nslot in entry 7 of a RAM table, the one devices 6 then use: $0B07 when B = $FF, else $0B0F.\nEntry: B = $FF for the $0B00 table, DE = the card's name (a length byte, then the text), C =\nits type byte.\nExit: A = 0, or $FD when no card matches.","comment":"Entry 7 of table $0B00"},
        "1235": {"comment":"B = $FF?"},
        "1238": {"comment":"No: entry 7 of table $0B08"},
        "1241": {"synopsis":"Looks for the card and stores its slot in the entry."},
        "1242": {"comment":"Find the card: C = its slot"},
        "1247": {"comment":"Not found"},
        "1248": {"comment":"Store the slot"},
        "1250": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $73: stores C in entry B of a RAM table ($0B00 when D = $FF, else $0B08),\nprovided B and C are both 0-6 and neither entry is unused ($FF).\nEntry: D = $FF for table $0B00, B = the entry to set, C = the value (an entry number).\nExit: A = 0, or $FE when refused.","comment":"Table $0B00"},
        "1253": {"comment":"D = $FF?"},
        "1256": {"comment":"No: table $0B08"},
        "1259": {"synopsis":"Checks entry B, then entry C, and stores C in entry B."},
        "1260": {"comment":"Entries 0-6 only"},
        "1263": {"comment":"B above 6?"},
        "1268": {"comment":"HL = entry B"},
        "1270": {"comment":"Unused ($FF)?"},
        "1273": {"comment":"DE = the table"},
        "1274": {"comment":"Keep entry B's address"},
        "1276": {"comment":"C above 6?"},
        "1279": {"comment":"HL = entry C"},
        "1284": {"comment":"Unused ($FF)?"},
        "1287": {"comment":"Entry B's address"},
        "1288": {"comment":"Store C in it"},
        "1289": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $70 points at this RET (the end of SET_RAM_ENTRY): the call does nothing and\nreturns A = the code, $70, which the dispatcher treats as an error."},
        "1290": {"synopsis":"Refuses the change: error $FE."},
        "1291": {"comment":"Error $FE"},
        "1294": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $74 (also called by SET_CARD_ENTRY): looks for a card by name and type in the\nfour 48-byte slot records at $0040, $0070, $00A0 and $00D0 in RAM. A record matches when it\nstarts with the same length byte and text as DE's name, and its byte 7 equals C.\nEntry: DE = the name (a length byte, then the text), C = the type byte.\nExit: A = 0 and C = the slot (0-3) when found; A = $FD otherwise. DE preserved.\nChanges: B, HL, IX."},
        "1295": {"comment":"The first slot record"},
        "1299": {"comment":"4 slots"},
        "1301": {"synopsis":"Compares the name in one slot record.","comment":"HL = the record"},
        "1305": {"comment":"B = the length + 1: the length byte too"},
        "1308": {"synopsis":"Compares the length byte and the text, byte by byte."},
        "1309": {"comment":"Same as the record's?"},
        "1316": {"comment":"The name matched"},
        "1320": {"comment":"Byte 7 of the record"},
        "1325": {"comment":"The type wanted?"},
        "1328": {"comment":"C = the slot, 0-3"},
        "1332": {"comment":"Found"},
        "1333": {"synopsis":"Restores DE and returns."},
        "1335": {"synopsis":"The name differed: drops the saved counter."},
        "1336": {"synopsis":"Moves IX to the next 48-byte record and restores DE; after four slots fails with $FD.","comment":"48 bytes a record"},
        "1341": {"comment":"The name again"},
        "1345": {"comment":"Error $FD: no such card"},
        "1349": {"synopsis":"The 16 CRTC register values, written R15 first down to R0 by CRTC_INIT_LOOP: R15/R14 $0EFF\n(the cursor address, which places the frame interrupt), R13/R12 0 (the start address),\nR11-R9 3, R8 0, R7 $42, R6 $3C, R5 2, R4 $4D, R3 $32, R2 $4B, R1 $40 (64 characters a row),\nR0 $63 (100 a row in all)."},
        "1365": {"synopsis":"Four routine words, called in turn by BOOT_IO_INIT's loop with C = $70: this chip's\nGFX_BOOT, KBD_INIT, SND_NOP and CAS_BOOT."},
        "1373": {"synopsis":"The device table read by SC_CALL_DEVICE: one word per device 0-7, each pointing at the\ndevice's function list (a count byte, then that many routine words). Device 6's word is\n$0000: SC_TEST_DEV6 sends it to EXTH before the table is read."},
        "1389": {"synopsis":"Sends a block one byte at a time through a byte routine (the block function of devices 2 and\n4).\nEntry: HL = the routine that sends the byte in C, DE = the buffer, BC = the length (not 0).\nExit: A = 0 when all are sent; the routine's error code when it fails.\nThe error $FA when the buffer address is above the top of RAM ($0B19) returns with three\nwords still pushed, so it returns to the address in BC: a flaw of the ROM.","comment":"HL = the buffer, DE = the routine"},
        "1390": {"synopsis":"Sends the byte at HL through the routine in DE, then moves on until BC runs out."},
        "1395": {"comment":"DE = the buffer"},
        "1396": {"comment":"$0B19: the top of RAM"},
        "1400": {"comment":"Is the buffer above it?"},
        "1402": {"comment":"Error $FA"},
        "1406": {"comment":"Leaves 3 words pushed (returns to BC)"},
        "1407": {"comment":"C = the byte to send"},
        "1408": {"comment":"HL = the routine"},
        "1409": {"comment":"Send it"},
        "1415": {"comment":"Failed?"},
        "1417": {"comment":"Next byte, count down"},
        "1419": {"comment":"All sent: A = 0"},
        "1422": {"synopsis":"Jumps to HL: the block routines call it to call their byte routine."},
        "1423": {"synopsis":"Fills a block one byte at a time from a byte routine (the block function of device 2).\nEntry: HL = the routine that returns a byte in C, DE = the buffer, BC = the length (not 0).\nExit: A = 0 when the block is full; the routine's error code when it fails.\nAs in BLOCK_OUT, the error $FA when the buffer is above the top of RAM ($0B19) returns to\nthe address in BC."},
        "1428": {"comment":"$0B19: the top of RAM"},
        "1432": {"comment":"Is the buffer above it?"},
        "1434": {"comment":"Error $FA"},
        "1438": {"comment":"Leaves 3 words pushed (returns to BC)"},
        "1439": {"comment":"Read a byte into C"},
        "1442": {"comment":"Keep the result"},
        "1443": {"comment":"The byte, kept in A' over the pops"},
        "1447": {"comment":"The result again"},
        "1448": {"comment":"Failed?"},
        "1450": {"comment":"The byte"},
        "1451": {"comment":"Into the buffer"},
        "1454": {"comment":"Next byte, count down"},
        "1457": {"comment":"Full: A = 0"},
        "1460": {"synopsis":"The character set: 96 glyphs for character codes 32 (space) to 127, 10 bytes each, one byte\na pixel row, top row first, bit 7 leftmost. The drawing code at $0CDB and $1427-$1439 finds\ncode c's glyph at $C474 + 10c (so code 32 is the first); a code with bit 7 set takes its\nglyph from RAM at $0740 + 10 x (c - 128) instead."},
        "2420": {"synopsis":"Device 0's function list (the graphics screen): a count byte (13) and the words of functions\n0-C, reached by RST $30 codes $00-$0C through DEVICE_TABLE. 0 does nothing (GFX_NOP), 1\ndraws a character at the graphics position, 2 draws a block of them, 3 is GFX_CURSOR_AT, 4\nsets the screen mode, 5 clears the screen, 6 moves or draws to a point, 7 does it relative,\n8 plots the current point and puts the pen down, 9 lifts the pen, A is GFX_PAINT, B is\nGFX_DEF_CHAR (copies 10 bytes to a RAM glyph at $0740) and C sets the palette."},
        "2447": {"synopsis":"Called first by the graphics functions: pages the video RAM in and has the caller's own\nreturn restore the paging. Saves the page byte at $0003, writes $50 to it and to port 02h\n(U0 in page 0, the video RAM in page 2, SYS in page 3), then plants GFX_PAGE_BACK as the\ncaller's return address and goes on in the caller.\nChanges: HL, AF.","comment":"The caller's return address"},
        "2448": {"comment":"Keep the old page byte"},
        "2452": {"comment":"U0, video RAM, SYS"},
        "2457": {"comment":"Page it in"},
        "2459": {"comment":"The return address back"},
        "2460": {"comment":"Return through GFX_PAGE_BACK"},
        "2464": {"comment":"Go on in the caller"},
        "2465": {"synopsis":"Where a graphics function returns to after GFX_PAGE_IN: restores the page byte at $0003 and\nport 02h it saved, keeping A and the flags (the function's result) in the alternate AF\nmeanwhile, then returns to the function's caller.","comment":"Keep the result"},
        "2466": {"comment":"The old page byte"},
        "2470": {"comment":"Paged back"},
        "2472": {"comment":"The result back"},
        "2473": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $00 (device 0 function 0): does nothing (it is the RET of GFX_PAGE_BACK)."},
        "2474": {"synopsis":"Reads word number A of the table at HL.\nEntry: HL = the table, A = the index.\nExit: DE = the word, HL = its high byte's address.","comment":"Two bytes a word"},
        "2478": {"comment":"The word's address"},
        "2479": {"comment":"Its low byte"},
        "2483": {"synopsis":"Checks a graphics X coordinate.\nEntry: BC = X.\nExit: carry set if X is above $03FF (1023).","comment":"The largest X"},
        "2486": {"comment":"Carry clear"},
        "2487": {"comment":"X above 1023?"},
        "2490": {"synopsis":"Checks a graphics Y coordinate.\nEntry: DE = Y.\nExit: carry set if Y is above $03BF (959); a negative Y counts as above.","comment":"The largest Y"},
        "2494": {"comment":"Y above 959?"},
        "2497": {"synopsis":"Turns a graphics X into a pixel column: shifts HL right (mode + 1) times, the mode from\n$0B73 (0: 512 pixels, 1: 256, 2: 128 across). SHIFT_HL_RIGHT shifts HL right B times.\nExit: HL = the column, A = the mode, B = 0.","comment":"The mode"},
        "2501": {"comment":"Mode + 1 shifts"},
        "2502": {"synopsis":"Shifts HL right one bit; loops B times.","comment":"Halve HL"},
        "2509": {"synopsis":"Prepares a line: the line style byte to $0B83 (GET_LINE_STYLE), then falls into PAPER_SETUP.","comment":"The line style"},
        "2512": {"synopsis":"Prepares drawing: the paper's colour byte to $0B93, DE = $0040 (one pixel row of video RAM),\nthen INK_SETUP: IY = the routine that puts a byte under a mask (SELECT_PUT), the ink's\ncolour byte to $0B94, and GET_PIXEL_PTR.","comment":"The paper's colour byte"},
        "2518": {"comment":"One pixel row of video RAM"},
        "2521": {"comment":"The put routine to IY","synopsis":"Entry without the paper byte: IY = the put routine for the put mode, then the ink's colour byte to\n$0B94 (STORE_INK_BYTE) and the current pixel (GET_PIXEL_PTR)."},
        "2524": {"comment":"The ink's colour byte"},
        "2527": {"synopsis":"Stores A as the ink's colour byte ($0B94), then GET_PIXEL_PTR. Also called by device 0\nfunction A ($0D72)."},
        "2530": {"synopsis":"Loads the current pixel: C = its mask (from $0B75), HL = its byte in video RAM (from $0B76).","comment":"The pixel's mask"},
        "2534": {"comment":"The pixel's byte"},
        "2538": {"synopsis":"The colour monitor's default palette, written to ports 60h-63h by GFX_SET_PALETTE: black,\nbright green, bright red, bright blue (I/G/R/B in bits 6/4/2/0)."},
        "2542": {"synopsis":"The default palette without a colour monitor (port 59h bit 6 clear): black, bright white,\nbright green, bright red."},
        "2546": {"synopsis":"The boot calls this (word at BOOT_INIT_TABLE): sets 4-colour mode (C = 1) through\nGFX_SET_MODE, which it falls into.","comment":"4-colour mode"},
        "2548": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $04 (device 0 function 4): sets the screen mode and resets the graphics state.\n1. Refuses a mode above 2 with A = $F7.\n2. Clears $0B4B (put mode), $0B4E (paper colour) and $0B50 (glyph flags); sets $0B4C (line\n   style) to 1 and $0B4D (ink colour) to 1, or in 16-colour mode to $0C on a colour monitor\n   and $0F otherwise (port 59h bit 6).\n3. Stores the mode in $0B73, clears the screen (GFX_CLS), puts the mode into bits 1-0 of\n   $0B13 and port 06h.\n4. Picks PAL_COLOUR or PAL_MONO by port 59h bit 6 and falls into GFX_SET_PALETTE.\nEntry: C = the mode (0: 2-colour, 1: 4-colour, 2: 16-colour).\nExit: A = 0, or $F7 for a bad mode.","comment":"The mode"},
        "2549": {"comment":"Above 2?"},
        "2551": {"comment":"The error code"},
        "2554": {"comment":"Clear the put mode, paper and flags"},
        "2564": {"comment":"Line style 1 (solid)"},
        "2568": {"comment":"16-colour mode?"},
        "2571": {"comment":"Port 59h bit 6: a colour monitor?"},
        "2575": {"comment":"Ink 15 without one"},
        "2579": {"comment":"Ink 12 with one"},
        "2581": {"synopsis":"Stores the default ink colour.","comment":"The default ink"},
        "2584": {"comment":"The mode for the drawing code"},
        "2589": {"comment":"Clear the screen in the new mode"},
        "2593": {"comment":"The mode into bits 1-0"},
        "2594": {"comment":"Port 06h's copy"},
        "2602": {"comment":"Set the video mode"},
        "2604": {"comment":"A colour monitor?"},
        "2608": {"comment":"The palette without one"},
        "2613": {"comment":"The palette with one"},
        "2616": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $0C (device 0 function C): sets the palette, writing four bytes to ports\n60h-63h. Not paged: DE is read in the caller's memory.\nEntry: DE = the four palette bytes (I/G/R/B in bits 6/4/2/0).\nExit: A = 0, DE = the last byte's address.","comment":"Palette 0"},
        "2620": {"comment":"Palette 1"},
        "2624": {"comment":"Palette 2"},
        "2628": {"comment":"Palette 3"},
        "2631": {"comment":"No error"},
        "2633": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $05 (device 0 function 5): clears the graphics screen. Calls ED_RESET (which\nclears RAM $0E48-$0E67 and fills $0100-$06FF with spaces), fills the video RAM $8000-$BBFF\nwith the paper's colour byte, lifts the pen (GFX_PEN_UP) and moves to (0, 0) through\nGFX_MOVE_TO.\nExit: A = 0.","comment":"Page the video RAM in"},
        "2636": {"comment":"Clear the RAM areas at $0E48 and $0100"},
        "2639": {"comment":"The paper's colour byte"},
        "2642": {"comment":"The video RAM"},
        "2648": {"comment":"Its first byte"},
        "2649": {"comment":"The rest: 15360 bytes in all"},
        "2654": {"comment":"Pen up; A = 0"},
        "2657": {"comment":"Position (0, 0)"},
        "2661": {"synopsis":"Moves the graphics position to (BC, DE) without drawing and works out its pixel. GFX_SET_X\nsets only X (BC) and keeps Y.\n1. Stores X at $0B7C and Y at $0B7E.\n2. The pixel column (X shifted by the mode) to $0B78; the mask of that pixel in its byte to\n   $0B75, from PIXEL_MASKS rotated right by the column's low 3 bits.\n3. The pixel row (959 - Y) / 4 to $0B7A (0 at the top, 239 at the bottom).\n4. $8000 + row x 64 + X / 16 to $0B76: the pixel's byte in video RAM.\nEntry: BC = X (0-1023), DE = Y (0-959, 0 at the bottom); not checked here.\nExit: A = 0.","comment":"Store Y"},
        "2665": {"comment":"Store X","synopsis":"Sets X to BC and keeps Y, then works out the pixel as GFX_MOVE_TO does."},
        "2669": {"comment":"X"},
        "2673": {"comment":"X / 16: the byte column"},
        "2678": {"comment":"Keep it; X again"},
        "2679": {"comment":"The pixel column"},
        "2682": {"comment":"Store it"},
        "2685": {"comment":"The mode as an index"},
        "2686": {"comment":"The column's low byte"},
        "2687": {"comment":"The leftmost pixel's mask"},
        "2691": {"comment":"Column AND 7, + 1"},
        "2695": {"comment":"The mask"},
        "2696": {"comment":"Hides the RRCA on the first pass"},
        "2697": {"synopsis":"Rotates the mask right; loops B times. The LD E,$0F before it hides this RRCA on the first\npass, so the mask turns (column AND 7) times."},
        "2700": {"comment":"The pixel's mask"},
        "2703": {"comment":"Y"},
        "2707": {"comment":"The top"},
        "2711": {"comment":"959 - Y"},
        "2713": {"comment":"/ 4"},
        "2718": {"comment":"The pixel row (0 at the top)"},
        "2721": {"comment":"Row x 64"},
        "2729": {"comment":"The byte column"},
        "2731": {"comment":"The video RAM"},
        "2735": {"comment":"The pixel's byte"},
        "2738": {"comment":"No error"},
        "2740": {"synopsis":"The leftmost pixel's mask in a video byte, by mode, read by GFX_MOVE_TO: $80 for 2-colour\n(one bit a pixel), $88 for 4-colour (bits 7 and 3), $AA for 16-colour (bits 7, 5, 3, 1)."},
        "2743": {"synopsis":"Draws one dot of a glyph through the put routine in IY.\nEntry: carry = the glyph bit, A = the glyph flags ($0B50), C = mask, HL = byte.\nA set bit is drawn in ink unless flag bit 0 is set; a clear bit in paper unless flag bit 1\nis set.","comment":"A clear glyph bit?"},
        "2745": {"comment":"Ink dots wanted?"},
        "2749": {"comment":"No: skip the dot"},
        "2750": {"synopsis":"Draws one dot of a line: in ink if carry is set (the line style's bit), in paper if not.\nPLOT_INK and PLOT_PAPER draw the dot at HL/C in ink ($0B94) or paper ($0B93) through IY.","comment":"A clear style bit: paper"},
        "2752": {"comment":"The ink's colour byte","synopsis":"Puts the ink's colour byte ($0B94) through the put routine in IY."},
        "2755": {"comment":"Put it"},
        "2757": {"synopsis":"A clear glyph bit: skipped if flag bit 1 is set.","comment":"Paper dots wanted?"},
        "2760": {"comment":"The paper's colour byte","synopsis":"Puts the paper's colour byte ($0B93) through the put routine in IY."},
        "2763": {"comment":"Put it"},
        "2765": {"synopsis":"Put routines, reached through PUT_ROUTINES and IY; each puts the colour byte A into the bits\nof (HL) that the mask C selects. PUT_AND: ANDs the colour into those bits.","comment":"Colour AND screen"},
        "2766": {"synopsis":"PUT_SET: replaces those bits with the colour.","comment":"Screen bits flipped where they differ"},
        "2767": {"synopsis":"PUT_XOR: XORs the colour into those bits.","comment":"Only the masked bits"},
        "2768": {"comment":"Merged into the screen"},
        "2771": {"synopsis":"PUT_OR: ORs the colour into those bits.","comment":"The colour's masked bits"},
        "2772": {"comment":"Added to the screen"},
        "2775": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $07 (device 0 function 7): GFX_DRAW_TO relative to the current position\n(ADD_GFX_POS), which it falls into.\nEntry: BC, DE = the X and Y offsets.","comment":"Make the point absolute"},
        "2778": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $06 (device 0 function 6): moves to (BC, DE); with the pen down ($0B74 not 0)\ndraws a line there from the current position.\n1. Refuses a point off the screen with A = $F9 and carry.\n2. Pen up: just GFX_MOVE_TO.\n3. Keeps the old pixel column and row, prepares the colours and style (LINE_SETUP), then\n   moves to the new point.\n4. Works out |dx| and |dy| in pixels and their signs (ABS_DIFF); the diagonal step for the\n   signs from DIAG_STEPS to $0B86, the straight step along the longer axis from AXIS_STEPS\n   to $0B84.\n5. Draws major + 1 dots from the old point, a Bresenham line (LINE_STEP), each dot in ink or\n   paper by the next bit of the line style.\nEntry: BC = X (0-1023), DE = Y (0-959).\nExit: A = 0, or $F9 with carry.","comment":"Page the video RAM in"},
        "2781": {"comment":"The error code"},
        "2783": {"comment":"X off the screen?"},
        "2786": {"comment":"Or Y?"},
        "2789": {"comment":"Yes: error"},
        "2790": {"comment":"The pen"},
        "2793": {"comment":"Up?"},
        "2794": {"comment":"Yes: just move"},
        "2797": {"comment":"Keep the new point"},
        "2798": {"comment":"The old pixel column"},
        "2802": {"comment":"The old pixel row"},
        "2808": {"comment":"Style, colours, put routine, old pixel"},
        "2811": {"comment":"The new point back"},
        "2812": {"comment":"Move to it"},
        "2815": {"comment":"The new pixel column"},
        "2819": {"comment":"The new pixel row"},
        "2823": {"comment":"The sign bits start at 0"},
        "2824": {"comment":"HL = the new row"},
        "2825": {"comment":"The old row"},
        "2826": {"comment":"|dy|; up?"},
        "2829": {"comment":"The old column"},
        "2830": {"comment":"Keep |dy|"},
        "2831": {"comment":"The new column"},
        "2833": {"comment":"|dx|; left?"},
        "2836": {"comment":"Keep |dx|"},
        "2837": {"comment":"The diagonal step for the signs"},
        "2843": {"comment":"Keep it"},
        "2847": {"comment":"|dx|"},
        "2848": {"comment":"|dy|"},
        "2850": {"comment":"Is Y the longer axis?"},
        "2855": {"comment":"No: HL = |dy|, DE = |dx|"},
        "2856": {"synopsis":"HL = the shorter delta, DE = the longer.","comment":"BC = the shorter delta"},
        "2858": {"comment":"Keep the longer one"},
        "2859": {"comment":"Y longer into the index"},
        "2861": {"comment":"Shorter - longer"},
        "2864": {"comment":"Longer / 2"},
        "2868": {"comment":"The first error"},
        "2870": {"comment":"The straight step"},
        "2876": {"comment":"Keep it"},
        "2880": {"comment":"The error"},
        "2881": {"comment":"Shorter - longer"},
        "2882": {"comment":"Back to the pixel"},
        "2883": {"comment":"The longer delta; the pixel's byte kept"},
        "2884": {"comment":"High byte + 1 rounds"},
        "2885": {"comment":"Low byte + 1 dots in the first"},
        "2888": {"comment":"Round count aside"},
        "2889": {"comment":"The pixel's byte"},
        "2890": {"comment":"The first dot without a step"},
        "2892": {"synopsis":"Next 256 dots: the high byte's count is in the alternate A.","comment":"The round count back"},
        "2893": {"synopsis":"Steps to the next dot of the line.","comment":"To the next dot"},
        "2896": {"synopsis":"Turns the line style byte one bit and draws the dot by it; loops for the low byte's count.","comment":"The line style"},
        "2899": {"comment":"Its next bit to carry"},
        "2903": {"comment":"Draw the dot by it"},
        "2906": {"comment":"Next dot of the round"},
        "2908": {"comment":"The round count"},
        "2909": {"comment":"Another round?"},
        "2913": {"synopsis":"The absolute difference of two values and its sign.\nEntry: HL, DE.\nExit: HL = |HL - DE|; A shifted left with carry (set when HL < DE) into bit 0.","comment":"HL - DE"},
        "2915": {"comment":"Not negative?"},
        "2917": {"comment":"The negative difference"},
        "2918": {"comment":"Carry is set: 1 - d - 1 = -d"},
        "2923": {"synopsis":"Shifts the sign into A.","comment":"HL < DE into bit 0"},
        "2925": {"synopsis":"Fetches the line style: byte ($0B4C) - 1 (AND 15) of LINE_STYLES to $0B83.","comment":"The line style number"},
        "2928": {"comment":"From 0"},
        "2932": {"comment":"The styles"},
        "2938": {"comment":"The style's bits"},
        "2943": {"synopsis":"The 16 line styles, read by GET_LINE_STYLE: each a bit pattern used from bit 7 round and\nround, a set bit drawn in ink and a clear one in paper. Style 1 ($FF) is solid."},
        "2959": {"synopsis":"Sets IY to the put routine for the put mode in bits 1-0 of $0B4B, from PUT_ROUTINES. Keeps\nDE.","comment":"Keep DE"},
        "2960": {"comment":"The put mode"},
        "2965": {"comment":"The put routines"},
        "2971": {"comment":"To IY"},
        "2976": {"synopsis":"The put routines by put mode, read by SELECT_PUT: 0 PUT_SET, 1 PUT_OR, 2 PUT_AND, 3 PUT_XOR."},
        "2984": {"synopsis":"One Bresenham step of a line. The alternate set holds the error (HL), the shorter delta (BC)\nand shorter - longer (DE). A negative error grows by BC and the dot takes the straight step\nat $0B84; otherwise by DE and the diagonal step at $0B86.","comment":"The error"},
        "2986": {"comment":"Negative?"},
        "2989": {"comment":"Error += shorter"},
        "2990": {"comment":"Back to the pixel"},
        "2991": {"comment":"The straight step"},
        "2997": {"synopsis":"The diagonal step.","comment":"Error += shorter - longer"},
        "2999": {"comment":"The diagonal step"},
        "3005": {"synopsis":"Steps that move the pixel (HL = byte, C = mask, DE = $0040) one place, reached through\nDIAG_STEPS and AXIS_STEPS and by GFX_CHAR_OUT. A row up is HL - 64, a row down HL + 64; a\npixel left turns C left and past bit 7 goes to the previous byte, a pixel right the other\nway. STEP_UP_LEFT: up and left.","comment":"A row up"},
        "3008": {"synopsis":"STEP_LEFT: one pixel left.","comment":"The mask a pixel left"},
        "3010": {"comment":"Still in this byte?"},
        "3011": {"comment":"The byte to the left"},
        "3013": {"synopsis":"STEP_UP_RIGHT: one pixel right, then up.","comment":"The mask a pixel right"},
        "3015": {"comment":"Still in this byte?"},
        "3017": {"comment":"The byte to the right"},
        "3018": {"synopsis":"STEP_UP: one row up.","comment":"A row up"},
        "3022": {"synopsis":"STEP_DOWN_LEFT: one pixel left, then down.","comment":"The mask a pixel left"},
        "3024": {"comment":"Still in this byte?"},
        "3026": {"comment":"The byte to the left"},
        "3027": {"synopsis":"STEP_DOWN: one row down.","comment":"A row down"},
        "3029": {"synopsis":"STEP_DOWN_RIGHT: down, then right.","comment":"A row down"},
        "3030": {"synopsis":"STEP_RIGHT: one pixel right.","comment":"The mask a pixel right"},
        "3032": {"comment":"Still in this byte?"},
        "3033": {"comment":"The byte to the right"},
        "3035": {"synopsis":"The diagonal steps, read by GFX_DRAW_TO, by index (up x 2 + left): STEP_DOWN_RIGHT,\nSTEP_DOWN_LEFT, STEP_UP_RIGHT, STEP_UP_LEFT."},
        "3043": {"synopsis":"The straight steps, read by GFX_DRAW_TO, by index (up x 4 + left x 2 + Y longer): right,\ndown, left, down, right, up, left, up."},
        "3059": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $08 (device 0 function 8): plots the current point in ink, then puts the pen\ndown ($0B74 = $FF), so moves draw lines. The LD H,$AF at $0BFE hides GFX_PEN_UP's XOR A.\nExit: A = 0.","comment":"Page the video RAM in"},
        "3062": {"comment":"Put routine, ink, pixel"},
        "3065": {"comment":"Plot it in ink"},
        "3068": {"comment":"Pen down"},
        "3070": {"comment":"Hides the XOR A"},
        "3071": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $09 (device 0 function 9): lifts the pen ($0B74 = 0), so moves no longer draw.\nExit: A = 0."},
        "3072": {"comment":"The pen"},
        "3075": {"comment":"No error"},
        "3077": {"synopsis":"The paper colour ($0B4E) as a colour byte (COLOUR_BYTE), also in B.","comment":"The paper colour"},
        "3080": {"comment":"As a colour byte"},
        "3085": {"synopsis":"The ink colour ($0B4D) as a colour byte, through COLOUR_BYTE, which it falls into.","comment":"The ink colour"},
        "3088": {"synopsis":"Turns a colour number into a colour byte for the mode in $0B73: the colour in every pixel of\nthe byte. 2-colour: bit 0 gives $00 or $FF; 4-colour: bit 0 into the high nibble and bit 1\ninto the low (CB_4_COLOUR); 16-colour: the 4 bits in both pixels, bit 3 in bits 7 and 6 down\nto bit 0 in bits 1 and 0 (CB_16_COLOUR).\nEntry: A = the colour.\nExit: A = the byte.\nChanges: HL.","comment":"L = the mode"},
        "3091": {"comment":"4-colour?"},
        "3095": {"comment":"16-colour?"},
        "3099": {"comment":"2-colour: bit 0"},
        "3101": {"comment":"To carry"},
        "3102": {"comment":"$00 or $FF"},
        "3104": {"comment":"Two bits","synopsis":"4-colour mode: colour bit 1 fills the low nibble and bit 0 the high nibble ($00 or $F0, OR $00\nor $0F)."},
        "3106": {"comment":"Bit 0 to carry"},
        "3107": {"comment":"Kept in L"},
        "3109": {"comment":"Bit 1 to carry"},
        "3110": {"comment":"$00 or $FF"},
        "3111": {"comment":"Bit 0 back"},
        "3113": {"comment":"Into bit 7"},
        "3114": {"comment":"Bit 7 into bits 6-4"},
        "3121": {"comment":"Four bits","synopsis":"16-colour mode: each of the four colour bits fills two bits of the byte (CB_16_LOOP)."},
        "3123": {"comment":"4 bits"},
        "3125": {"comment":"Next bit, low first","synopsis":"Doubles each colour bit into L, bit 0 first, so bit 3 ends in bits 7-6 and bit 0 in bits 1-0;\nloops 4 times."},
        "3126": {"comment":"Into L"},
        "3128": {"comment":"And copied"},
        "3130": {"comment":"All four?"},
        "3133": {"comment":"The byte"},
        "3135": {"synopsis":"Reads the colour number of the current pixel from video RAM for the mode in $0B73; the\ninverse of COLOUR_BYTE (2-colour: 0/1; 4-colour: RP_4_COLOUR; 16-colour: RP_16_COLOUR). Used\nby device 0 function A (GFX_PAINT).\nExit: A = the colour, C = the mask, HL = the byte.","comment":"The pixel's mask and byte"},
        "3138": {"comment":"The mode"},
        "3142": {"comment":"The pixel's bits"},
        "3144": {"comment":"4-colour?"},
        "3148": {"comment":"16-colour?"},
        "3152": {"comment":"2-colour: any bit set?"},
        "3154": {"comment":"To bit 0"},
        "3158": {"comment":"High nibble bit to carry","synopsis":"4-colour mode: colour bit 0 from the high nibble, bit 1 from the low nibble."},
        "3160": {"comment":"Kept in C"},
        "3162": {"comment":"The low nibble"},
        "3164": {"comment":"Its bit to carry"},
        "3166": {"comment":"Into bit 0: colour bit 1"},
        "3167": {"comment":"The high nibble's bit back"},
        "3169": {"comment":"Into bit 0: colour bit 0"},
        "3170": {"comment":"The colour"},
        "3173": {"comment":"Bits 7-6 set?","synopsis":"16-colour mode: one colour bit from each pair of bits, bits 7-6 first."},
        "3175": {"comment":"Into bit 0"},
        "3178": {"comment":"Bits 5-4 set?"},
        "3183": {"comment":"Bits 3-2 set?"},
        "3188": {"comment":"Bits 1-0 set?"},
        "3191": {"comment":"The colour"},
        "3194": {"synopsis":"Adds the graphics position to an offset: BC += X ($0B7C), DE += Y ($0B7E).","comment":"X"},
        "3197": {"comment":"+ the X offset"},
        "3200": {"comment":"Y"},
        "3203": {"comment":"+ the Y offset"},
        "3206": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $02 (device 0 function 2): draws a block of characters with GFX_CHAR_OUT.\nEntry: DE = the characters (read in the caller's memory), BC = how many (at least one).","comment":"HL = the characters"},
        "3207": {"synopsis":"Draws the next character; loops until BC runs out."},
        "3209": {"comment":"The next character"},
        "3210": {"comment":"Draw it"},
        "3215": {"comment":"Past it; count down"},
        "3217": {"comment":"All done?"},
        "3220": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $01 (device 0 function 1): draws a character on the graphics screen at the\ngraphics position.\n1. Codes below 32: LF (10) GFX_LF, CR (13) GFX_CR, the rest are ignored; codes from $E0 too.\n2. A pixel's width in coordinates goes to $0B82 (2, 4 or 8 by mode); a character is 8 pixels\n   wide and 10 rows high, its top row 36 units below the position. Nothing is drawn if it\n   would go below the screen; if it would go past the right edge a CR and LF come first.\n3. The glyph: codes $20-$7F from the ROM table at $05B4, codes $80-$DF from RAM at $0740 (10\n   bytes each, bit 7 the left pixel).\n4. Draws the 10 rows, every dot in ink or paper by the glyph flags $0B50 (GLYPH_DOT) through\n   the put routine.\n5. Moves X right by 8 pixels, or past the right edge does a CR and LF.\nEntry: C = the character.\nExit: A = 0.","comment":"Page the video RAM in"},
        "3223": {"comment":"The character"},
        "3224": {"comment":"A control code?"},
        "3228": {"comment":"Line feed?"},
        "3233": {"comment":"Carriage return?"},
        "3238": {"comment":"Others are ignored"},
        "3240": {"synopsis":"Printable: a pixel's width in coordinates for the mode, and 7 of them for the check.","comment":"From $E0 up?"},
        "3242": {"comment":"Ignored"},
        "3245": {"comment":"Keep the character"},
        "3246": {"comment":"The top row is 36 below"},
        "3249": {"comment":"The mode"},
        "3253": {"comment":"Doubled mode + 1 times"},
        "3256": {"synopsis":"Doubles A; loops (mode + 1) times."},
        "3259": {"comment":"A pixel's width in coordinates"},
        "3262": {"comment":"x 7: to the last dot"},
        "3267": {"comment":"The X offset"},
        "3269": {"comment":"The character's top row and right edge"},
        "3272": {"comment":"Below the screen?"},
        "3275": {"comment":"Yes: nothing"},
        "3276": {"comment":"Past the right edge?"},
        "3281": {"comment":"Yes: a new line first"},
        "3287": {"synopsis":"Prepares the colours, the put routine and the pixel (the alternate set holds it from here).","comment":"Paper, put routine, ink, pixel"},
        "3290": {"comment":"The character back"},
        "3291": {"comment":"The ROM glyphs ($05B4 is code 32)"},
        "3294": {"comment":"Code $80 up?"},
        "3298": {"comment":"The RAM glyphs at $0740"},
        "3301": {"synopsis":"The glyph's address: code x 10 plus the table's base.","comment":"Without bit 7"},
        "3305": {"comment":"Code"},
        "3307": {"comment":"x 4"},
        "3309": {"comment":"x 5"},
        "3310": {"comment":"x 10"},
        "3311": {"comment":"The glyph"},
        "3312": {"comment":"10 rows"},
        "3314": {"comment":"The glyph flags"},
        "3318": {"comment":"Undone by the INC"},
        "3319": {"synopsis":"Next glyph row: its byte in C; loops for 10 rows.","comment":"Next row"},
        "3320": {"comment":"Its bits"},
        "3321": {"comment":"To the pixel"},
        "3322": {"comment":"8 dots"},
        "3324": {"comment":"The first without a step"},
        "3326": {"synopsis":"Next dot of the row: one pixel right.","comment":"One pixel right"},
        "3329": {"synopsis":"Draws the dot for the glyph's next bit (bit 7 first); loops 8 times."},
        "3330": {"comment":"Next glyph bit"},
        "3332": {"comment":"The flags"},
        "3334": {"comment":"Draw the dot"},
        "3337": {"comment":"Next dot"},
        "3339": {"comment":"A row down"},
        "3342": {"comment":"7 pixels"},
        "3344": {"synopsis":"Back 7 pixels to the character's left edge.","comment":"One pixel left"},
        "3349": {"comment":"To the glyph"},
        "3350": {"comment":"Next row"},
        "3352": {"comment":"A pixel's width"},
        "3355": {"comment":"x 8: one character"},
        "3359": {"comment":"X past the character"},
        "3362": {"comment":"Past the right edge?"},
        "3365": {"comment":"No: just move"},
        "3367": {"comment":"After the CR a line feed"},
        "3371": {"synopsis":"Carriage return on the graphics screen: X to 0 (GFX_SET_X). GCR_SET_X sets X to BC.\nExit: A = 0.","comment":"X = 0"},
        "3374": {"comment":"Set X; Y unchanged","synopsis":"Sets X to BC, Y unchanged (GFX_SET_X)."},
        "3377": {"synopsis":"Line feed on the graphics screen: Y goes down by 40 (one character row) if a character still\nfits below; otherwise nothing.\nExit: A = 0.","comment":"76: this row and the next one"},
        "3380": {"comment":"Y - 76"},
        "3383": {"comment":"No error"},
        "3384": {"comment":"Below the screen?"},
        "3387": {"comment":"Yes: stay"},
        "3388": {"comment":"One character row down"},
        "3391": {"comment":"X unchanged"},
        "3397": {"comment":"Move there"},
        "3400": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $0A (device 0, function A): fills the area around the graphics position with\nthe ink colour - BASIC's PLOT PAINT (the SYS high chip's $E75A). The area is every pixel\nconnected to the one under the graphics position that has its colour (the region colour).\nThe fill works span by span: a span is a run of region pixels on one scanline, filled at\nonce, and kept on the machine stack as a seed of three words (leftmost byte address, B bytes\nto the row's end / C pixel mask, span word) so the rows above and below it are searched\nlater. The span word holds the length in pixels in bits 13-0; bit 6 of its high byte says\nthe span lies within the span it was found from, and bit 7 then says in which direction (0\nbelow, 1 above), so that row need not be searched again.\n1. Pages the video RAM in, saves SP at $0B88 (the seed stack's base) and plots with PUT_XOR.\n2. Reads the region colour's fill byte into $0B8A and puts ink XOR region in $0B94 (XORing a\n   region pixel with it gives ink); returns at once if the ink is the region colour.\n3. Pushes a zero word (the end mark) and fills the start pixel's own row (FILL_ROW, once),\n   which pushes its span.\n4. FILL_NEXT_SEED pops seeds until the end mark, searching the row below (B = 2) then the\n   row above (B = 1) of each.\nEntry: the graphics position ($0B76/$0B75 its byte and mask), ink $0B4D, mode $0B73.\nExit: A = 0. The stack never goes below $0B17 (FILL_ROOM): when it would, unneeded seeds are\nsqueezed out, and if none can go the current search is abandoned, so the fill can stop\nshort.\nChanges: all main and alternate registers, IY; RAM $0B88-$0B94.","comment":"Video RAM in, paging restored on return"},
        "3403": {"comment":"The seed stack starts here"},
        "3407": {"comment":"Spans are filled by XOR"},
        "3411": {"comment":"Colour of the pixel under the position"},
        "3414": {"comment":"As a fill byte"},
        "3417": {"comment":"The region colour"},
        "3421": {"comment":"Ink fill byte"},
        "3424": {"comment":"Ink XOR region"},
        "3428": {"comment":"Ink already the region colour?"},
        "3429": {"comment":"No parent span for the first row"},
        "3432": {"comment":"The end mark of the seeds"},
        "3433": {"comment":"Byte column = X / 16"},
        "3442": {"comment":"$0B94 = ink XOR region; byte and mask"},
        "3445": {"comment":"Bytes from here to the row end"},
        "3450": {"comment":"Row address to search"},
        "3453": {"comment":"Its mask and byte count"},
        "3457": {"comment":"Only this one row"},
        "3461": {"synopsis":"Takes the next seed off the stack (returns when it is the end mark) and makes the row below\nit the row to search, with the seed's span as the parent; a seed on the bottom row goes\nstraight to the row above (FILL_ROW_DONE).","comment":"Span word"},
        "3464": {"comment":"The end mark: done"},
        "3465": {"comment":"Mask and byte count"},
        "3466": {"comment":"The parent span"},
        "3473": {"comment":"Its byte address"},
        "3474": {"comment":"One scanline down"},
        "3481": {"comment":"Two rows: below, then above"},
        "3483": {"comment":"Below the last scanline?"},
        "3491": {"synopsis":"Moves the row counter in B to the alternate set and falls into FILL_ROW."},
        "3492": {"synopsis":"Searches one row (address $0B8D, mask and byte count $0B8B) under the parent span ($0B91)\nfor region pixels, filling and pushing each span found. Skips the row when the parent's\nflags say it is the row the parent came from. If the parent's first pixel is a region pixel\nthe span through it is filled both ways; FILL_GAP then walks the rest of the parent's length\nfor more.","comment":"Room for another seed?"},
        "3495": {"comment":"No: give up this row"},
        "3497": {"comment":"The parent span word"},
        "3501": {"comment":"Its length without the flags"},
        "3505": {"comment":"Pixels of the parent left to search"},
        "3509": {"comment":"Parent within its own parent?"},
        "3513": {"comment":"Its direction bit to bit 0"},
        "3515": {"comment":"Against this row's direction"},
        "3517": {"comment":"Opposite: the row it came from"},
        "3522": {"synopsis":"Starts at the parent's first pixel: a pixel not of the region colour goes to FILL_GAP;\notherwise the pixels to its left are filled first (FILL_LEFT_LOOP), counting them in DE.","comment":"The parent's first pixel"},
        "3529": {"comment":"Region colour?"},
        "3534": {"comment":"No: look further right"},
        "3536": {"comment":"Bytes from here to the row start"},
        "3541": {"comment":"Pixels filled leftwards"},
        "3544": {"comment":"From the pixel on the left"},
        "3546": {"synopsis":"Fills region pixels leftwards until another colour or the row's start.","comment":"Region colour?"},
        "3551": {"comment":"No: the span starts right of it"},
        "3553": {"comment":"Make it ink"},
        "3557": {"synopsis":"One pixel left, into the previous byte when the mask wraps.","comment":"Mask one pixel left"},
        "3561": {"comment":"Wrapped: previous byte"},
        "3564": {"synopsis":"Goes back one pixel to the span's leftmost and turns B back into bytes to the row end.","comment":"Bytes to the row end again"},
        "3569": {"comment":"Back one pixel to the right"},
        "3575": {"synopsis":"Pushes the span's leftmost address and mask and the left count, then fills rightwards from\nthe parent's first pixel (FILL_RIGHT).","comment":"Seed: leftmost byte"},
        "3576": {"comment":"Its mask and byte count"},
        "3577": {"comment":"Pixels filled on the left"},
        "3578": {"comment":"From the parent's first pixel"},
        "3585": {"comment":"Fill rightwards, DE = count"},
        "3588": {"comment":"Left count; keep the right end"},
        "3589": {"comment":"Any pixel filled on the left?"},
        "3593": {"comment":"Bit 7: the span sticks out left"},
        "3595": {"comment":"Length = left + right","synopsis":"The span's length: the pixels filled leftwards (bit 7 set when there were any) plus those filled\nrightwards."},
        "3597": {"synopsis":"Finishes a span's seed word: the span is flagged as within its parent (bit 6, direction in\nbit 7) when it sticks out neither left nor right; the parent's remaining length goes down by\nthe pixels filled, and the row is done when nothing is left of it.","comment":"Keep the span's right end"},
        "3598": {"comment":"Parent pixels left"},
        "3602": {"comment":"Less the ones filled"},
        "3604": {"comment":"DE = right end"},
        "3605": {"comment":"HL = span length, remainder pushed"},
        "3606": {"comment":"Past the parent's end?"},
        "3609": {"comment":"Sticks out on the left?"},
        "3611": {"comment":"Yes: drop bit 7, no flags"},
        "3613": {"comment":"Past the parent: no flags"},
        "3615": {"comment":"Carry = this row's direction bit"},
        "3621": {"comment":"Bit 7 direction, bit 6 within"},
        "3622": {"comment":"Into the span word","synopsis":"Puts the direction (bit 7) and within-parent (bit 6) flags into the span word's high byte."},
        "3624": {"synopsis":"Leaves the span word on the stack (the seed is complete) and the parent's remainder in HL."},
        "3625": {"comment":"Seed done; HL = parent remainder"},
        "3626": {"comment":"Parent used up: row done"},
        "3630": {"comment":"DE = remainder, HL = right end"},
        "3631": {"synopsis":"Walks right along the parent span over pixels not of the region colour (DE pixels left); the\nfirst region pixel starts a new span (FILL_NEW_SPAN), the parent's end finishes the row.\nChecks first that the stack has room for a seed.","comment":"Room for another seed?"},
        "3634": {"comment":"No: give up this row"},
        "3637": {"comment":"Count with E then D"},
        "3642": {"comment":"Region colour?","synopsis":"Steps over the parent span's pixels that are not region colour, looking for the next one that is."},
        "3647": {"comment":"Yes: a new span"},
        "3649": {"comment":"Parent pixels left?"},
        "3652": {"synopsis":"One pixel right, into the next byte when the mask wraps.","comment":"Mask one pixel right"},
        "3656": {"comment":"Wrapped: next byte"},
        "3660": {"synopsis":"A region pixel inside the parent: saves the parent's remainder, pushes the pixel's address\nand mask and fills rightwards from it; FILL_SPAN_WORD finishes the seed.","comment":"Count back to a plain number"},
        "3665": {"comment":"Parent pixels left","synopsis":"Stores the parent pixels still to search ($0B8F) and starts the new seed with its byte address."},
        "3669": {"comment":"Seed: leftmost byte"},
        "3671": {"comment":"Its mask and byte count"},
        "3672": {"comment":"Fill rightwards, DE = count"},
        "3675": {"comment":"The count, as the span length"},
        "3678": {"synopsis":"The low count ran out: goes on while the high byte is not exhausted.","comment":"High byte of the count"},
        "3682": {"synopsis":"Ends a row: counts down the rows still to search for this seed (B in the alternate set);\nafter the row below comes the row above (one scanline above the seed), unless it is above\nthe top scanline. Then the next seed."},
        "3683": {"synopsis":"Entered from FILL_NEXT_SEED when the row below is off the screen: counts that row as\nsearched and goes on to the row above.","comment":"Rows left for this seed?"},
        "3688": {"comment":"From the row below"},
        "3691": {"comment":"Two scanlines up"},
        "3698": {"comment":"Above the top scanline ($8000)?"},
        "3703": {"comment":"Search the row above"},
        "3706": {"synopsis":"Fills region pixels rightwards from the pixel at HL/C until another colour or the row's end\n(B bytes), by XORing each with $0B94.\nEntry: HL byte, C mask, B bytes to the row end.\nExit: DE = pixels filled; HL/C = the first pixel not filled.\nChanges: A, B, C, DE, HL.","comment":"Pixels filled"},
        "3709": {"comment":"Region colour?","synopsis":"The fill loop: XORs each region-colour pixel with $0B94 and counts it in DE, until a pixel of\nanother colour or the row's end."},
        "3714": {"comment":"No: span ends here"},
        "3716": {"comment":"Ink XOR region on this pixel"},
        "3722": {"comment":"Mask one pixel right"},
        "3726": {"comment":"Wrapped: next byte"},
        "3730": {"synopsis":"Makes sure the stack has room for another seed: returns with carry clear while SP - 22 is\nnot below $0B17 (the lowest address the stack may use, also checked by the SYS high chip at\n$FCAA). Otherwise squeezes the seed stack: walks the seeds from the oldest (just under the\nend mark at $0B88) and keeps, packed towards the base, only those whose span still has a\nregion pixel directly below or above it (FILL_SEED_USED); then moves its own 10 stacked\nbytes (four saved pairs and the return address) after them and sets SP there.\nExit: carry set when no seed could be dropped (no room).\nChanges: AF only."},
        "3732": {"comment":"SP less 22 bytes for a seed"},
        "3736": {"comment":"Below the stack's floor?"},
        "3742": {"comment":"No: room, carry clear"},
        "3748": {"comment":"The seed stack's base"},
        "3751": {"comment":"The oldest seed's top byte"},
        "3754": {"comment":"Kept seeds go here (DE)"},
        "3756": {"synopsis":"The squeeze loop: BC = HL = the seed being looked at, DE = where the next kept seed goes.","comment":"The seed looked at"},
        "3758": {"comment":"Top of this routine's own 10 bytes"},
        "3763": {"comment":"Reached them?"},
        "3767": {"comment":"No: judge this seed"},
        "3769": {"comment":"Move the 10 bytes up as well"},
        "3774": {"comment":"The new SP"},
        "3778": {"comment":"Moved at all?"},
        "3781": {"comment":"Carry: nothing dropped"},
        "3785": {"synopsis":"Restores DE and HL and returns (carry set when the squeeze dropped nothing)."},
        "3788": {"synopsis":"Judges a seed: keeps it when a region pixel lies under its span on the scanline below\n(within the screen) or, failing that, on the scanline above; otherwise drops it.","comment":"The seed: HL byte, DE length"},
        "3791": {"comment":"The scanline below"},
        "3795": {"comment":"Below the last one?"},
        "3800": {"comment":"Region pixel below the span?"},
        "3803": {"comment":"Yes: keep the seed"},
        "3805": {"comment":"The seed again","synopsis":"No region pixel below the seed's span: tries the scanline above it."},
        "3809": {"comment":"The scanline above"},
        "3811": {"comment":"Above the top one?"},
        "3817": {"comment":"Region pixel above the span?"},
        "3820": {"synopsis":"Copies the seed's 6 bytes to the kept ones (Z) or skips them (NZ)."},
        "3824": {"comment":"No region pixel: drop it"},
        "3826": {"comment":"Keep: copy it down to DE"},
        "3831": {"synopsis":"Drops the seed: steps past its 6 bytes without copying them, then goes on with the next."},
        "3832": {"comment":"Skip the seed"},
        "3837": {"synopsis":"FILL_READ_SEED with the seed pointer still in the alternate HL: switches sets first."},
        "3838": {"synopsis":"Reads the seed whose top byte the alternate HL points at.\nExit: HL = its byte address, DE = its span length (flags cleared), A = its pixel mask, BC =\n64 (one scanline).\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL.","comment":"The seed's top byte"},
        "3841": {"comment":"Address high"},
        "3846": {"comment":"Mask"},
        "3848": {"comment":"Span word high, flags off"},
        "3854": {"comment":"A = mask"},
        "3855": {"comment":"One scanline"},
        "3860": {"synopsis":"Scans the pixels from HL (mask A) rightwards for about DE of them.\nExit: Z when one has the region colour ($0B8A).\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL.","comment":"C = mask"},
        "3861": {"comment":"B = region colour fill byte"},
        "3865": {"synopsis":"Tests one pixel: returns Z when it has the region colour, else counts it off."},
        "3866": {"comment":"Region colour?"},
        "3869": {"comment":"Pixels left?"},
        "3872": {"comment":"Mask one pixel right","synopsis":"Moves the mask one pixel right, to the next byte when it wraps."},
        "3876": {"comment":"Wrapped: next byte"},
        "3879": {"comment":"High byte of the count","synopsis":"The count's low byte ran out: counts the high byte down, returning NZ when it is spent."},
        "3884": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $0B (device 0, function B): defines a character - BASIC's SET CHARACTER. Copies\n10 bytes (the glyph's rows) from DE to the RAM glyph of code C at $0740 + (C - $80) * 10.\nEntry: C = character code ($80-$DF), DE = the 10 bytes.\nExit: A = 0; A = $F8 when the code is below $80 or from $E0 (nothing copied).\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL."},
        "3885": {"comment":"From $E0?"},
        "3889": {"comment":"Sign = code from $80"},
        "3890": {"comment":"Error code for a bad code","synopsis":"A = $F8 (error) for a code below $80 or from $E0; otherwise copies the glyph to RAM."},
        "3892": {"comment":"Below $80 or from $E0: error"},
        "3893": {"comment":"RAM glyph number 0-$5F"},
        "3897": {"comment":"Times 10"},
        "3903": {"comment":"RAM glyphs at $0740"},
        "3908": {"comment":"10 rows"},
        "3913": {"comment":"No error"},
        "3915": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $03 (device 0, function 3): moves the graphics position to a character cell,\ncolumn B and row C counted from 1 at the top left; 0 leaves that coordinate as it is. X =\n(B-1) * the character width (16/32/64 in modes 0/1/2), Y = 959 - (C-1) * 40, the cell's top.\nThe banner code calls it at $1A2E-$1A52.\nEntry: B = column, C = row.\nExit: A = 0; A = $F9 with carry when the cell is off the screen.\nChanges: all main and alternate registers but IX/IY."},
        "3916": {"comment":"X = 0 for column 0 or 1"},
        "3919": {"comment":"Column given?"},
        "3923": {"comment":"Column - 1"},
        "3924": {"comment":"Mode + 4: shift for the width","synopsis":"X = (column - 1) shifted by the mode + 4: 16, 32 or 64 coordinates a character."},
        "3930": {"comment":"Times 16/32/64","synopsis":"The shift loop for X."},
        "3933": {"comment":"Keep X"},
        "3934": {"comment":"40 coordinates a text row, up"},
        "3937": {"comment":"Y of row 1's top"},
        "3940": {"comment":"Row given and above 1?"},
        "3947": {"comment":"One text row down","synopsis":"Y down 40 coordinates for each row below row 1."},
        "3950": {"comment":"DE = Y","synopsis":"The cell's X in BC and Y in DE; a column or row given as 0 keeps the current coordinate."},
        "3951": {"comment":"BC = X"},
        "3953": {"comment":"The current X and Y"},
        "3962": {"comment":"B column, C row as given"},
        "3963": {"comment":"Row 0?"},
        "3967": {"comment":"Yes: keep the current Y"},
        "3971": {"comment":"Column 0?","synopsis":"Column 0: keeps the current X."},
        "3975": {"comment":"Yes: keep the current X"},
        "3979": {"comment":"Error code: off the screen","synopsis":"A = $F9 with carry when the position is off the screen; otherwise moves there (GFX_MOVE_TO)."},
        "3981": {"comment":"X above 1023?"},
        "3985": {"comment":"Y above 959?"},
        "3989": {"comment":"Move there"},
        "3992": {"synopsis":"Device 2's function list (the screen editor): a count byte (5) and the words of functions\n0-4, reached by RST $30 codes $20-$24 through its word at $0561 in DEVICE_TABLE: 0 the\ncursor blink (ED_BLINK), 1 a character in or out (ED_CHAR), 2 a block (ED_BLOCK), 3 sets the\ncursor (ED_SET_CURSOR), 4 marks the input start (ED_MARK_START)."},
        "4003": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $20 (device 2, function 0): the cursor blink, called on every frame interrupt\nby INT_POLL_DEVS while bit 2 of $0B10 is clear, which the editor's key wait (ED_READ_CHAR)\narranges. Counts $0E48 up: the 20th call draws the cursor glyph over the cell at the cursor\n($0E49) and sets the counter to $80; 20 calls later (at $94) the cell is put back and the\ncounter starts again from 0. The glyph shows the lock state in $0B66: $7F with no lock, the\nRAM glyphs $9E (bit 0 set), $9F (bit 1 set) or $8F (any other lock).\nChanges: AF, BC, D, HL and what the drawing routines change.","comment":"U0, video RAM, SYS"},
        "4007": {"comment":"The blink counter"},
        "4010": {"comment":"BC = cursor row and column"},
        "4015": {"comment":"Reached $94?"},
        "4018": {"comment":"Yes: put the cell back"},
        "4020": {"comment":"Reached $14?"},
        "4023": {"comment":"Counter to $80"},
        "4024": {"comment":"The lock state"},
        "4027": {"comment":"Glyph $7F with no lock"},
        "4034": {"comment":"Bit 0 set?"},
        "4039": {"comment":"Bit 1 set?"},
        "4042": {"comment":"Any other lock"},
        "4044": {"comment":"Draw the cursor glyph","synopsis":"Draws the cursor glyph in D at the cursor's cell."},
        "4048": {"comment":"Counter back to 0","synopsis":"Sets the blink counter back to 0 and puts the cell under the cursor back."},
        "4049": {"comment":"Put the saved cell back"},
        "4052": {"synopsis":"Resets the screen editor (called by GFX_CLS, device 0's function 5): clears its RAM\n$0E48-$0E67 (blink counter, read mode, input start, the per-row lengths), puts the cursor at\nrow 1, column 1, fills the text buffer $0100-$06FF with spaces and copies the current mode's\nentry of ED_MODE_TABLE behind a JP opcode to $0E68-$0E6C (the glyph writer, the text width\nand bytes per character).\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL.","comment":"Editor RAM"},
        "4055": {"comment":"32 bytes"},
        "4058": {"synopsis":"The clearing loop: 32 bytes from $0E48."},
        "4062": {"comment":"Cursor row 1, column 1"},
        "4068": {"comment":"The text buffer"},
        "4074": {"comment":"1536 bytes: 24 rows of 64"},
        "4077": {"comment":"Spaces"},
        "4081": {"comment":"Mode * 4: its table entry"},
        "4091": {"comment":"JP, then the entry's 4 bytes"},
        "4096": {"comment":"JP opcode at $0E68"},
        "4098": {"comment":"Handler word, columns, bytes per cell"},
        "4100": {"comment":"To $0E69-$0E6C"},
        "4103": {"synopsis":"Three 4-byte entries, one per video mode ($0B73 = 0, 1, 2), copied by $0FF1 to $0E69-$0E6C\nbehind a JP at $0E68: the glyph writer's address, the number of text columns, and the bytes\none character takes on a scanline. Mode 0: GLYPH_MODE0, 64 columns, 1 byte; mode 1:\nGLYPH_MODE1, 32, 2; mode 2: GLYPH_MODE2, 16, 4."},
        "4115": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $24: marks the cursor as the start of the next line read. The editor's read\n(ED_READ_CHAR) then returns the logical line from this position rather than from its first\ncolumn, so a prompt printed before it is not read back.\nExit: A = 0, $0E4D = $80.","comment":"The cursor (row, column)"},
        "4118": {"comment":"Remember it as the input start"},
        "4121": {"comment":"Bit 7: a start is set"},
        "4125": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $23: moves the cursor.\nEntry: B = column (1 to the width, 0 keeps it), C = row (1-24, 0 keeps it).\nExit: A = 0, the read mode in $0E4D cleared; A = $F6 when a value is out of range (cursor\nunchanged).","comment":"L = row, H = column now"},
        "4128": {"comment":"Column given?"},
        "4132": {"comment":"The text width"},
        "4135": {"comment":"Column beyond it?"},
        "4138": {"comment":"Take the new column"},
        "4139": {"synopsis":"Takes the row from C unless it is 0.","comment":"Row given?"},
        "4143": {"comment":"24 rows"},
        "4145": {"comment":"Row beyond them?"},
        "4148": {"comment":"Take the new row"},
        "4149": {"synopsis":"Stores the cursor and falls into ED_CLEAR_MODE."},
        "4152": {"synopsis":"Clears the read mode in $0E4D (no input start, no line being returned) and returns A = 0."},
        "4153": {"synopsis":"Stores A as the read mode in $0E4D (bit 0: a line's characters are being returned, bit 7: an\ninput start is set) and returns A = 0."},
        "4156": {"comment":"No error"},
        "4158": {"synopsis":"A value is out of range: returns A = $F6.","comment":"Error code"},
        "4161": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $22 / $A2: transfers a block through the editor.\nEntry: DE = the buffer, BC = the count; the sign of the code (S flag on entry) picks the\ndirection. Code $22 writes the bytes one by one by handing ED_WRITE_CHAR to BLOCK_OUT; code\n$A2 reads characters into the buffer by handing ED_READ_CHAR to BLOCK_IN.","comment":"Keep BC, DE, HL"},
        "4162": {"comment":"Colour bytes for this mode"},
        "4166": {"comment":"Code bit 7 clear: write each byte"},
        "4172": {"comment":"Bit 7 set: read each character"},
        "4178": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $21 / $A1: one character through the editor. Code $21 (S clear) writes the\ncharacter in C at the cursor (ED_WRITE_CHAR); code $A1 falls into ED_READ_CHAR.","comment":"Colour bytes for this mode"},
        "4181": {"comment":"Code bit 7 clear: write C"},
        "4184": {"synopsis":"Reads one character through the screen editor. The user moves around the screen and edits it\nuntil RETURN; then the logical line under the cursor (from the input start if ED_MARK_START\nset one) is returned one character per call, read from the text buffer, and ended by $0D.\nExit: A = 0 and C = the character; C = $1B when ESC was pressed; A <> 0 when the keyboard\ncall failed.\n1. While a line is being returned ($0E4D bit 0), continue at EDREAD_NEXT.\n2. Forget the input start if the cursor is before it (on a row above it, or left of it on\n   its row).\n3. Wait for a key with the cursor blinking, then act on it: ESC ends, RETURN starts\n   returning the line, any other key is put on the screen (ED_PUT_CHAR) and the wait\n   repeats.","comment":"U0 in page 0 (the text buffer), video RAM in 2"},
        "4186": {"comment":"Record the page byte"},
        "4191": {"comment":"Read mode"},
        "4194": {"comment":"Returning a line already?"},
        "4198": {"synopsis":"The key loop: checks the input start against the cursor, then waits for a key.","comment":"The cursor row's entry"},
        "4201": {"comment":"Cursor: C = row, B = column"},
        "4206": {"comment":"The input start: E = row, D = column"},
        "4210": {"synopsis":"Is the cursor at or after the input start? A row above the start forgets it; the start's own\nrow compares the columns; a row below walks up the rows that run on, and keeps the start in\nevery case.","comment":"Row above the start's row?"},
        "4214": {"comment":"Below it: check the row above"},
        "4216": {"comment":"Same row: column at or after the start's?"},
        "4220": {"synopsis":"The cursor is before the input start: the read mode is cleared."},
        "4225": {"synopsis":"One row up while the row above runs on into this one; a row that does not run on ends the\ncheck with the start kept.","comment":"Off the top?"},
        "4228": {"comment":"The row above's entry"},
        "4229": {"comment":"Does it run on into this row?"},
        "4231": {"comment":"Any column of a row above is before the cursor"},
        "4235": {"synopsis":"Saves the picture under the cursor, lets the frame routine blink it, and waits for a key."},
        "4236": {"comment":"Save the cell under the cursor"},
        "4239": {"comment":"Blink counter: cursor shown on the next frame"},
        "4244": {"comment":"Bit 2 clear: device 2's frame function runs"},
        "4249": {"comment":"Wait for a key (code $91): C = the key"},
        "4251": {"comment":"Stop the blinking"},
        "4255": {"comment":"Put back the cell under the cursor"},
        "4264": {"comment":"Keyboard error?"},
        "4265": {"comment":"The key"},
        "4266": {"comment":"ESC?"},
        "4270": {"comment":"RETURN?"},
        "4274": {"comment":"Anything else goes on the screen"},
        "4279": {"synopsis":"Writes the character in C at the cursor. CR moves the cursor to column 1; LF moves it to the\nrow after the logical line, keeping the column; any other code is put on the screen or\nobeyed as a control code (ED_PUT_CHAR).\nExit: A = 0, the read mode in $0E4D cleared (through ED_CLEAR_MODE).","comment":"U0 in page 0 (the text buffer), video RAM in 2"},
        "4286": {"comment":"Leave through ED_CLEAR_MODE"},
        "4290": {"comment":"The character"},
        "4291": {"comment":"CR?"},
        "4295": {"comment":"Column 1"},
        "4301": {"synopsis":"LF moves below the logical line; anything else goes to ED_PUT_CHAR.","comment":"LF?"},
        "4305": {"comment":"The cursor"},
        "4309": {"synopsis":"Steps down while the row runs on into the next one."},
        "4310": {"comment":"Does row C run on?"},
        "4316": {"comment":"The logical line's last row"},
        "4317": {"comment":"Keep the column"},
        "4318": {"comment":"Next row, column 1 (scrolls at the bottom)"},
        "4321": {"comment":"The old column"},
        "4322": {"comment":"Put it back"},
        "4326": {"synopsis":"Puts a printable character on the screen or obeys a control code."},
        "4331": {"synopsis":"RETURN: moves the cursor to the input start, or to the first column of the logical line when\nnone is set, and starts returning characters (falls into EDREAD_NEXT).","comment":"Bit 7 into carry: input start set?"},
        "4335": {"comment":"The input start"},
        "4339": {"comment":"None: the logical line's first column"},
        "4342": {"comment":"Read from there"},
        "4346": {"synopsis":"Returns the next character of the line from the text buffer and advances the cursor; past\nthe row's last used column the line ends with $0D.","comment":"Read position = the cursor"},
        "4350": {"comment":"The row's used length"},
        "4354": {"comment":"Past the last used column?"},
        "4357": {"comment":"The character's buffer address"},
        "4360": {"comment":"Next column"},
        "4361": {"comment":"Past the last column?"},
        "4367": {"comment":"Column 1 of the next row"},
        "4370": {"synopsis":"Stores the advanced cursor and returns the character with the read mode set to 1 (returning\na line)."},
        "4374": {"comment":"The character"},
        "4375": {"comment":"Mode: returning a line"},
        "4380": {"synopsis":"End of the line: the cursor goes to the next row and $0D is returned, with the read mode\ncleared.","comment":"Next row, column 1"},
        "4383": {"comment":"Return CR"},
        "4388": {"synopsis":"Puts the character in A on the screen at the cursor, or obeys it when it is a control code\n(below $20 or from $E0, ED_CONTROL). A printable one is stored in the text buffer, drawn,\nand the cursor advanced; past the last column the row is marked as running on and the next\nrow opened for it (ED_CONT_ROW).","comment":"Control code?"},
        "4396": {"comment":"Keep the character"},
        "4397": {"comment":"Its buffer address"},
        "4401": {"comment":"Into the text buffer"},
        "4402": {"comment":"The cursor"},
        "4407": {"comment":"Draw it"},
        "4411": {"comment":"The row's used length"},
        "4415": {"comment":"Does the row reach this column?"},
        "4418": {"comment":"No: it does now"},
        "4419": {"synopsis":"Advances the cursor; past the last column it goes to column 1 of the next row, which is\nopened if the row did not run on yet.","comment":"Next column"},
        "4420": {"comment":"The text width"},
        "4421": {"comment":"Still on the row?"},
        "4426": {"comment":"Column 1 of the next row"},
        "4429": {"comment":"Row already running on?"},
        "4431": {"comment":"No: mark it and open its next row"},
        "4434": {"synopsis":"Stores the cursor."},
        "4439": {"synopsis":"Obeys the control code in A: looks it up in ED_CTRL_TABLE and jumps to its handler with BC =\nthe cursor (C = row, B = column) and A = the text width. An unknown code is ignored.","comment":"The control code table"},
        "4442": {"comment":"11 entries"},
        "4444": {"synopsis":"Compares one table entry, picking up its handler address.","comment":"This entry's code?"},
        "4446": {"comment":"Its handler"},
        "4450": {"comment":"Found?"},
        "4452": {"comment":"Next entry"},
        "4454": {"comment":"Unknown code: ignored"},
        "4455": {"synopsis":"Jumps to the handler with the cursor in BC and the width in A.","comment":"C = row, B = column"},
        "4459": {"comment":"The text width"},
        "4464": {"synopsis":"The editor's control codes, read by ED_CONTROL: 11 entries of a code byte and a handler\nword. $13 left (CTL_LEFT), $04 right (CTL_RIGHT), $05 up (CTL_UP), $18 down (CTL_DOWN), $16\ninsert a character (CTL_INS_CHAR), $07 delete a character (CTL_DEL_CHAR), $08 back space\n(CTL_BACKSPACE), $19 delete the logical line (CTL_DEL_LINE), $0E insert a row\n(CTL_INS_LINE), $0B erase to the end of the line (CTL_ERASE_EOL), $09 tab (CTL_TAB)."},
        "4497": {"synopsis":"Control code $13: cursor left; from column 1 it goes to the last column of the row above\n(not above row 1).","comment":"Not at column 1?"},
        "4500": {"comment":"The last column"},
        "4501": {"synopsis":"Control code $05: cursor one row up, not above row 1.","comment":"One row up"},
        "4504": {"synopsis":"Control code $04: cursor right; past the last column it goes to column 1 of the next row\n(not below row 24).","comment":"Next column"},
        "4505": {"comment":"Still on the row?"},
        "4508": {"comment":"Column 1 of the next row"},
        "4510": {"synopsis":"Control code $18: cursor one row down, not below row 24."},
        "4511": {"comment":"Row 25?"},
        "4514": {"synopsis":"Leaves the cursor where it was when the new row is off the screen (0 or 25).","comment":"Off the screen: no move"},
        "4515": {"synopsis":"Stores the moved cursor."},
        "4520": {"synopsis":"Control code $0B: erases from the cursor to the end of the logical line. The row is blanked\non the screen and in the text buffer from the cursor, its length set to the column before\nthe cursor, and every row the line ran on to is deleted (ERASE_ROWS)."},
        "4521": {"comment":"Blank the picture to the row's end"},
        "4525": {"comment":"The cursor's buffer address"},
        "4528": {"comment":"Columns before the cursor"},
        "4529": {"comment":"The text width"},
        "4532": {"comment":"Columns to blank"},
        "4533": {"synopsis":"Fills the rest of the buffer row with spaces."},
        "4539": {"comment":"The row's entry; does it run on?"},
        "4542": {"comment":"Its length: up to the cursor, no run-on"},
        "4543": {"comment":"The next row"},
        "4544": {"comment":"Its entry"},
        "4545": {"synopsis":"Stops when the deleted row did not run on.","comment":"Was the line continued?"},
        "4546": {"synopsis":"Deletes row C, which the line ran on to; the rows below move up, so the same row and entry\nare checked again.","comment":"Its entry, before the rows move"},
        "4549": {"comment":"Delete row C"},
        "4553": {"comment":"Did it run on too?"},
        "4557": {"synopsis":"Control code $09: moves the cursor to the next tab stop (columns 9, 17, 25, ...). Past the\nlast column the row is marked full and running on, and the cursor goes to column 1 of the\nnext row, which is opened if needed. The row's length grows to cover the skipped columns.","comment":"Row's length; carry: runs on"},
        "4560": {"comment":"Keep the run-on flag"},
        "4561": {"comment":"Column - 1"},
        "4563": {"comment":"Next multiple of 8, plus 1"},
        "4568": {"comment":"The text width"},
        "4571": {"comment":"Stop on this row?"},
        "4574": {"comment":"Row full and running on"},
        "4577": {"comment":"Column 1 of the next row"},
        "4580": {"comment":"Did the row run on before?"},
        "4581": {"comment":"No: open its next row"},
        "4584": {"synopsis":"Extends the row's length to the column before the new cursor.","comment":"The cursor row's length"},
        "4588": {"comment":"Column before the stop"},
        "4589": {"comment":"Is the row that long?"},
        "4592": {"comment":"No: it is now"},
        "4593": {"synopsis":"Stores the cursor."},
        "4599": {"synopsis":"Control code $0E: inserts an empty row above the logical line under the cursor; the cursor\ngoes to its column 1.","comment":"To the logical line's first row"},
        "4605": {"synopsis":"Control code $19: deletes the logical line under the cursor, every row of it; the rows below\nmove up and the cursor is left at column 1 of the first row.","comment":"To the logical line's first row"},
        "4608": {"comment":"Its entry"},
        "4613": {"synopsis":"Control code $16: inserts a space at the cursor. Every character from the cursor to the end\nof the logical line moves one column right, each row's last character wrapping to column 1\nof the row below, on the screen and in the text buffer.\n1. Find the line's last row and count its rows (E); lengthen the last row by one.\n2. If that fills it, open a new row below for the line (ED_CONT_ROW), unless the line\n   already covers all 24 rows: then the last character is lost.\n3. From the last row up to the cursor row, shift the row right (the picture by\n   ROW_INSERT_CHAR, the buffer by LDDR) and bring in the character from the end of the row\n   above, or a space on the cursor row.","comment":"The row's length"},
        "4616": {"comment":"Cursor past the text?"},
        "4618": {"comment":"Row count"},
        "4622": {"synopsis":"Counts a row the line runs on to."},
        "4624": {"synopsis":"Walks down the logical line to its last row (C ends one past it).","comment":"Does the row run on?"},
        "4629": {"comment":"The last row is one longer"},
        "4630": {"comment":"The text width"},
        "4633": {"comment":"Is the last row full now?"},
        "4636": {"comment":"Rows + the row after the line"},
        "4638": {"comment":"All 24 rows used?"},
        "4642": {"comment":"Undo; the last character drops off"},
        "4643": {"comment":"Carry: line full"},
        "4646": {"synopsis":"The last row filled up: marks it as running on and opens the row below."},
        "4648": {"comment":"Open the next row"},
        "4651": {"comment":"The column back"},
        "4654": {"comment":"The last row again"},
        "4656": {"comment":"Its entry"},
        "4659": {"synopsis":"No character is lost: carry is cleared."},
        "4660": {"synopsis":"Shifts one row right, from the line's last row up to the cursor row (the counter in E\nreaches 0 on the cursor row).","comment":"Keep the line-full flag"},
        "4662": {"comment":"Previous row"},
        "4663": {"comment":"Zero on the cursor row"},
        "4668": {"comment":"Cursor row: shift from the cursor"},
        "4670": {"comment":"Other rows: shift the whole row"},
        "4672": {"synopsis":"Moves the row's picture right by one character from column B."},
        "4673": {"comment":"Shift the picture right"},
        "4677": {"comment":"The text width"},
        "4682": {"comment":"Row C, last column"},
        "4683": {"comment":"Its buffer address"},
        "4687": {"comment":"Line full?"},
        "4690": {"comment":"Drop the last character"},
        "4692": {"synopsis":"Moves the row's characters right in the text buffer.","comment":"Characters to move"},
        "4699": {"comment":"Nothing to move?"},
        "4704": {"synopsis":"Picks the character that wraps in from the end of the row above."},
        "4705": {"comment":"One row (64 bytes) back"},
        "4709": {"comment":"The row above's last character"},
        "4710": {"comment":"The cursor row?"},
        "4713": {"comment":"Then a space goes in"},
        "4715": {"synopsis":"Puts the wrapped character (or the space) at the row's start, or at the cursor."},
        "4716": {"comment":"Into the buffer"},
        "4720": {"comment":"The previous row's entry"},
        "4721": {"comment":"Until the cursor row is done"},
        "4723": {"comment":"Cursor unchanged"},
        "4728": {"synopsis":"Control code $08: moves the cursor left and deletes the character there (falls into\nCTL_DEL_CHAR). From column 1 it goes to the last character of the row above only when that\nrow runs on into this one.","comment":"Not at column 1?"},
        "4731": {"comment":"Row above; none above row 1"},
        "4734": {"comment":"Does the row above run on?"},
        "4737": {"comment":"No: nothing to delete"},
        "4738": {"comment":"Its last column"},
        "4739": {"synopsis":"Stores the moved cursor and deletes the character there."},
        "4743": {"synopsis":"Control code $07: deletes the character at the cursor. The rest of the logical line moves\none column left, each following row's first character going to the end of the row above; the\nlast row gets one shorter, and a run-on row left empty is deleted.","comment":"The row's length"},
        "4746": {"comment":"Cursor past the text?"},
        "4748": {"synopsis":"Shifts one row left from column B (carry clear on the cursor row, set on the rows after it).","comment":"Keep the first-row flag"},
        "4749": {"comment":"The row's used length"},
        "4752": {"comment":"Characters after column B"},
        "4753": {"comment":"Row empty?"},
        "4759": {"comment":"Shift the picture left"},
        "4766": {"comment":"Column B's buffer address"},
        "4769": {"comment":"A row after the cursor row?"},
        "4770": {"comment":"Its first character"},
        "4771": {"comment":"No: nothing to carry back"},
        "4773": {"comment":"To the end of the row above"},
        "4774": {"synopsis":"Moves the row's characters left in the text buffer."},
        "4777": {"comment":"Characters to move"},
        "4779": {"comment":"None?"},
        "4786": {"synopsis":"Blanks the row's last column and goes on with the next row while the line runs on.","comment":"Space into the last column"},
        "4790": {"comment":"The next row, from column 1"},
        "4793": {"comment":"This row's entry"},
        "4794": {"comment":"Does it run on?"},
        "4796": {"comment":"The next row's entry"},
        "4797": {"comment":"Carry: not the cursor row"},
        "4800": {"comment":"Back to the last row's entry"},
        "4801": {"comment":"It is one shorter"},
        "4803": {"synopsis":"A run-on row has nothing left: the row above stops running on and is one shorter, and the\nempty row is deleted.","comment":"The row above's entry"},
        "4805": {"comment":"No run-on any more"},
        "4807": {"comment":"Delete row C"},
        "4811": {"synopsis":"Marks the row whose entry is at HL as running on to the next row, and makes room for that\nrow.\nEntry: C = the next row. On row 25 the screen scrolls up instead (ED_SCROLL_UP); when the\nnext row already holds text, an empty row is inserted there (falls into ED_INSERT_ROW).","comment":"The row runs on"},
        "4814": {"comment":"Past row 24?"},
        "4818": {"comment":"The next row's entry"},
        "4820": {"comment":"Empty: use it as it is"},
        "4822": {"synopsis":"Inserts an empty row at row C: the picture from row C down moves one text row (10 scanlines)\nlower (SCR_INSERT_ROW), the row entries and the text buffer rows likewise, row 24 drops off,\nand row C is blanked. The input start moves down with its text."},
        "4823": {"comment":"Move the picture down"},
        "4828": {"comment":"Rows from C to 24"},
        "4831": {"comment":"Row entries: shift down by one"},
        "4843": {"comment":"The entry for row C"},
        "4845": {"comment":"Empty (C is 0 now)"},
        "4847": {"comment":"Row 24 does not run on"},
        "4849": {"comment":"Rows x 64 bytes"},
        "4856": {"comment":"Text buffer: end of row 23"},
        "4859": {"comment":"End of row 24"},
        "4864": {"comment":"Row C: 64 spaces"},
        "4868": {"synopsis":"Fills row C of the text buffer with spaces."},
        "4873": {"comment":"The input start's row"},
        "4877": {"comment":"Above the inserted row?"},
        "4879": {"comment":"No: it moved down"},
        "4881": {"synopsis":"Scrolls the screen up one row (deleting row 1) and puts the cursor at column 1 of row 24.","comment":"Delete row 1"},
        "4886": {"comment":"Row 24, column 1"},
        "4894": {"synopsis":"Deletes row A: the picture, the text buffer rows and the row entries below it move up one\nrow, and row 24 is left empty. The input start moves up with its text, and is forgotten when\nits row is the one deleted.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL.","comment":"Keep the row"},
        "4896": {"comment":"The input start's row"},
        "4899": {"comment":"Is it this row?"},
        "4900": {"comment":"Yes: forget the input start"},
        "4903": {"comment":"Start above the deleted row?"},
        "4905": {"comment":"Below: it moves up"},
        "4906": {"synopsis":"Moves the picture up, then the text buffer and the row entries.","comment":"Move the picture up"},
        "4910": {"comment":"The row"},
        "4911": {"comment":"Its entry"},
        "4915": {"comment":"Column 1 of the row"},
        "4918": {"comment":"Its buffer address"},
        "4922": {"comment":"The row below"},
        "4926": {"comment":"Rows below it"},
        "4930": {"comment":"None (row 24)?"},
        "4934": {"comment":"Rows x 64 bytes"},
        "4941": {"comment":"Move them up"},
        "4943": {"synopsis":"Fills the last buffer row with spaces."},
        "4945": {"synopsis":"Stores one space, backwards."},
        "4950": {"comment":"Rows below"},
        "4952": {"comment":"The deleted row's entry"},
        "4955": {"comment":"Nothing to move?"},
        "4958": {"comment":"Shift the entries up"},
        "4960": {"synopsis":"Row 24's entry: empty."},
        "4963": {"synopsis":"Moves the cursor to column 1 of the next row; on row 24 the screen scrolls up instead\n(ED_SCROLL_UP).\nEntry: C = the cursor row.","comment":"Row 24?"},
        "4976": {"synopsis":"Moves the cursor to column 1 of the first row of its logical line (up while the row above\nruns on).\nExit: BC = the new cursor (C = row, B = 1).","comment":"The cursor"},
        "4980": {"synopsis":"Steps up while the row above runs on into this one.","comment":"Clear carry for row 1"},
        "4982": {"comment":"Row above"},
        "4983": {"comment":"Does it run on?"},
        "4988": {"comment":"Back to the line's first row"},
        "4996": {"synopsis":"Returns in HL the text buffer address of the character at the cursor (falls into\nED_BUF_ADDR)."},
        "4999": {"synopsis":"Returns the text buffer address of a character position: $0100 + (row - 1) x 64 + (column -\n1). The buffer holds 24 rows of 64 bytes at $0100-$06FF (RAM U0, paged into page 0).\nEntry: L = row, H = column.\nExit: HL = the address, also kept in $0E4B.","comment":"Count from 0"},
        "5001": {"comment":"Column x 4"},
        "5004": {"comment":"Row's low 2 bits into A's top"},
        "5011": {"comment":"(row & 3) x 64 + column"},
        "5012": {"comment":"Row / 4 + 1: the high byte"},
        "5020": {"synopsis":"Returns the cursor row's entry (falls into ED_ROW_INFO_A)."},
        "5023": {"synopsis":"Returns row A's entry in the row table at $0E50-$0E67 (one byte per row: bits 6-0 the number\nof columns used, bit 7 set when the logical line runs on into the next row).\nEntry: A = row (1-24).\nExit: HL = the entry, A = the used length, carry = runs on.","comment":"$0E4F + row"},
        "5031": {"comment":"The entry"},
        "5032": {"comment":"Bit 7 into bit 0"},
        "5033": {"comment":"Clear carry"},
        "5034": {"comment":"Length in A, run-on in carry"},
        "5037": {"synopsis":"Draws one character in video mode 0 (one byte per 8 pixels): each glyph row byte becomes one\nscreen byte, ink where its bits are set and paper elsewhere. Reached through the JP at\n$0E68.\nEntry: HL = screen address, B = paper byte, C = ink xor paper; HL' = the glyph, B' = 10\nrows.","comment":"One scanline down"},
        "5041": {"synopsis":"Writes one glyph row.","comment":"Glyph row"},
        "5043": {"comment":"Ink on set bits, paper elsewhere"},
        "5046": {"comment":"Next scanline"},
        "5048": {"comment":"Next glyph row"},
        "5052": {"synopsis":"Draws one character in video mode 1 (two bytes per 8 pixels): each glyph row's high nibble\nbecomes the first screen byte and its low nibble the second, each pixel's bit set in both of\nits bit positions, then coloured with ink and paper. Entry as for GLYPH_MODE0."},
        "5053": {"synopsis":"Writes one glyph row as two screen bytes.","comment":"Glyph row"},
        "5056": {"comment":"Nibbles swapped"},
        "5060": {"comment":"High xor low nibble in both halves"},
        "5062": {"comment":"Kept for the second byte"},
        "5063": {"comment":"High nibble in both halves"},
        "5067": {"comment":"Coloured"},
        "5069": {"comment":"Second byte: low nibble in both halves"},
        "5072": {"comment":"Next scanline, back one byte"},
        "5077": {"comment":"Next glyph row"},
        "5081": {"synopsis":"Draws one character in video mode 2 (four bytes per 8 pixels): each screen byte takes two\nglyph bits, the first pixel in bits 7, 5, 3, 1 and the second in bits 6, 4, 2, 0, then\ncoloured with ink and paper. Entry as for GLYPH_MODE0."},
        "5082": {"synopsis":"Writes one glyph row as four screen bytes.","comment":"Glyph row"},
        "5084": {"comment":"First pixel's bit into carry"},
        "5086": {"comment":"$FF when it is set"},
        "5088": {"comment":"Second pixel's bit"},
        "5091": {"comment":"$55 bits from the second pixel"},
        "5095": {"comment":"Coloured"},
        "5098": {"comment":"Pixels 3 and 4"},
        "5113": {"comment":"Pixels 5 and 6"},
        "5128": {"comment":"Pixels 7 and 8"},
        "5143": {"comment":"Next scanline, back three bytes"},
        "5148": {"comment":"Next glyph row"},
        "5152": {"synopsis":"Draws the character in A at text position B (column), C (row) in the current ink and paper\ncolours ($0E95, $0E96). Glyphs are 10 bytes: codes $20-$7F from the ROM's table at $05B4\n(the first one is code $20), codes $80-$DF from RAM at $0740 + (code - $80) x 10. The mode's\nwriter is called through the JP at $0E68.","comment":"Keep the character"},
        "5153": {"comment":"The cell's screen address"},
        "5158": {"comment":"The code"},
        "5161": {"comment":"Code x 10"},
        "5167": {"comment":"Code from $80?"},
        "5169": {"comment":"ROM glyphs: $C474 + $20 x 10 = $C5B4"},
        "5174": {"comment":"RAM glyphs: $0240 + $80 x 10 = $0740"},
        "5177": {"synopsis":"Sets up the writer's registers and jumps to the mode's glyph writer.","comment":"The glyph's address"},
        "5178": {"comment":"10 rows"},
        "5181": {"comment":"Paper byte"},
        "5185": {"comment":"Ink xor paper"},
        "5189": {"comment":"The screen address"},
        "5190": {"comment":"The mode's glyph writer"},
        "5193": {"synopsis":"Works out the colour bytes for the current video mode: $0E96 = the paper byte (from the\ncolour in $0B4E, through PAPER_BYTE), $0E95 = the ink byte (from $0B4D, through INK_BYTE)\nxor paper. AF is kept.","comment":"Keep AF"},
        "5194": {"comment":"Paper colour as a byte"},
        "5200": {"comment":"Ink colour as a byte"},
        "5203": {"comment":"Xor paper, for the writers"},
        "5209": {"synopsis":"Returns the screen address of a character cell's top scanline: $8000 + (row - 1) x 640 +\n(column - 1) x the mode's bytes per character (1, 2, 4 for modes 0-2). Video RAM is in page\n2, 64 bytes a scanline, 10 scanlines a text row.\nEntry: B = column, C = row.\nExit: HL = the address.","comment":"Column from 0"},
        "5210": {"comment":"The video mode"},
        "5213": {"comment":"Mode 0: one byte per column"},
        "5216": {"synopsis":"Doubles the column's byte offset once per mode step."},
        "5221": {"synopsis":"Adds the row's offset: (row - 1) x 10 scanlines x 64 bytes, with bit 15 set.","comment":"Row from 0"},
        "5223": {"comment":"Row x 10"},
        "5228": {"comment":"x 64 into HL "},
        "5232": {"comment":"... with bit 15 set: $8000"},
        "5236": {"comment":"Plus the column's bytes"},
        "5239": {"synopsis":"Copies the picture of the character cell at B (column), C (row) to $0E6D (10 scanlines of\nthe mode's bytes per character), so the cursor can be drawn over it and taken off again."},
        "5242": {"comment":"The save area"},
        "5245": {"comment":"Bytes per character"},
        "5249": {"comment":"10 scanlines"},
        "5251": {"synopsis":"Copies one scanline of the cell."},
        "5258": {"comment":"Next scanline (64 bytes)"},
        "5265": {"synopsis":"Puts the picture saved by ED_SAVE_CELL back into the character cell at B (column), C (row)."},
        "5268": {"comment":"The save area"},
        "5271": {"comment":"Bytes per character"},
        "5275": {"comment":"10 scanlines"},
        "5277": {"synopsis":"Copies one scanline back into the cell."},
        "5286": {"comment":"Next scanline (64 bytes)"},
        "5293": {"synopsis":"Paints the picture from the character cell at B (column), C (row) to the right edge with the\npaper colour, all 10 scanlines of the row."},
        "5296": {"comment":"Paper byte"},
        "5300": {"comment":"Bytes after the cell's first"},
        "5305": {"comment":"None: one byte column only"},
        "5309": {"comment":"10 scanlines"},
        "5311": {"synopsis":"Fills one scanline from the cell's column to its end.","comment":"Same column on this scanline"},
        "5316": {"comment":"First byte, then copy it along"},
        "5326": {"comment":"The next scanline's start"},
        "5327": {"comment":"The column back in A"},
        "5331": {"synopsis":"The cell is the last byte of the scanline: only that byte column is painted."},
        "5339": {"synopsis":"Paints one byte per scanline."},
        "5344": {"synopsis":"Moves the picture of text rows C+1 to 24 up one text row (640 bytes) and paints row 24 with\nthe paper colour (falls into ED_CLEAR_LAST).\nEntry: C = the row that is overwritten.","comment":"Row 24: only blank it"},
        "5349": {"comment":"Column 1"},
        "5351": {"comment":"Row C's screen address"},
        "5355": {"comment":"Row 24's screen address"},
        "5359": {"comment":"Bytes up to row 24"},
        "5363": {"comment":"The row below (640 bytes on)"},
        "5367": {"comment":"Move the picture up"},
        "5369": {"synopsis":"Paints text row 24 ($B980-$BBFF) with the paper colour (falls into ED_FILL_ROW)."},
        "5375": {"synopsis":"Paints the 640 bytes (one text row) from HL with the paper colour.\nEntry: HL = the start, DE = HL + 1.","comment":"One text row"},
        "5378": {"comment":"The paper byte"},
        "5382": {"comment":"Fill the rest of the row"},
        "5385": {"synopsis":"Opens a blank text row at row C (1-24): rows C to 23 move one text row down (row 24 is lost)\nand row C is cleared with the paper fill byte; at row 24 only that row is cleared. The\nscreen is video RAM at $8000: 64 bytes per pixel line, 10 pixel lines (640 bytes) per text\nrow, 24 rows.\nEntry: C = the row.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL. Ends in the row clear at ED_FILL_ROW.","comment":"24 rows"},
        "5387": {"comment":"Rows below row C"},
        "5388": {"comment":"The bottom row: only clear it"},
        "5390": {"comment":"C = rows to move"},
        "5391": {"comment":"A = rows x 10 pixel lines"},
        "5396": {"comment":"BC = rows x 640 bytes"},
        "5404": {"comment":"End of row 23"},
        "5407": {"comment":"End of row 24, the last screen byte"},
        "5410": {"comment":"Move the rows one text row down"},
        "5412": {"comment":"HL = the start of row C"},
        "5413": {"comment":"DE = the next byte"},
        "5416": {"comment":"Clear row C"},
        "5418": {"synopsis":"Copies one character cell from HL to DE: 10 pixel lines of $0E6C bytes (the cell width), 64\nbytes apart.\nEntry: HL = the source cell, DE = the destination cell.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL.","comment":"The cell width in bytes"},
        "5422": {"synopsis":"Copies a block C bytes wide and 10 pixel lines high from HL to DE, the lines 64 bytes apart\n(CELL_COPY with the width given).","comment":"10 pixel lines"},
        "5424": {"synopsis":"Copies one pixel line, then steps both pointers to the next line."},
        "5427": {"comment":"BC = bytes per line"},
        "5429": {"comment":"Copy one pixel line"},
        "5431": {"comment":"64 bytes per pixel line"},
        "5433": {"comment":"Next destination line"},
        "5436": {"comment":"Next source line"},
        "5439": {"comment":"All 10 lines?"},
        "5442": {"synopsis":"Deletes the character at the cursor from the screen picture of one text row: the cells right\nof it move one cell left, then ROW_PULL_NEXT fills the row's last cell. Used by the screen\neditor's delete ($1297).\nEntry: B = the column, C = the row, HL = the row's length byte (bit 7 set when the line\ncontinues on the next row), A = characters right of the cursor with Z set when there are\nnone.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL.","comment":"No characters right of the cursor?"},
        "5444": {"comment":"Keep the length byte's address"},
        "5446": {"comment":"E = characters to move"},
        "5447": {"comment":"Bytes per character as a shift count"},
        "5450": {"comment":"One byte per character?"},
        "5453": {"synopsis":"Turns the character count into a byte count: one shift per step of $0B73.","comment":"Characters to bytes"},
        "5458": {"synopsis":"Moves the cells right of the cursor one cell left, line by line.","comment":"HL = the cursor cell"},
        "5461": {"comment":"C = bytes to move per line"},
        "5462": {"comment":"DE = the cursor cell"},
        "5463": {"comment":"One cell width on"},
        "5468": {"comment":"HL = the cell right of the cursor"},
        "5469": {"comment":"Move the rest of the row one cell left"},
        "5474": {"synopsis":"Fills a row's last cell after a delete: when the line continues on the next row (bit 7 of\nthe length byte at HL), that row's first character is copied up into it; otherwise the cell\nis cleared (CELL_CLEAR).\nEntry: HL = the row's length byte, C = the row.","comment":"B = the row's length, bit 7 = continued"},
        "5475": {"comment":"Does the line go on to the next row?"},
        "5480": {"comment":"B = the last column"},
        "5482": {"comment":"HL = the row's last cell"},
        "5485": {"comment":"DE = the destination"},
        "5487": {"comment":"The next row's"},
        "5488": {"comment":"...first column"},
        "5490": {"comment":"HL = its first cell"},
        "5493": {"comment":"Pull it up into the last cell"},
        "5496": {"synopsis":"Clears the character cell at column B, row C with the paper fill byte $0E96.\nEntry: B = the column, C = the row (both from 1).\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL.","comment":"HL = the cell"},
        "5499": {"comment":"10 pixel lines"},
        "5501": {"comment":"64 bytes per pixel line"},
        "5504": {"synopsis":"Clears one pixel line of the cell, then moves 64 bytes on.","comment":"The cell width in bytes"},
        "5508": {"comment":"The paper fill byte"},
        "5512": {"synopsis":"Writes the fill byte across the cell's width.","comment":"Blank one byte"},
        "5516": {"comment":"Back to the line's start"},
        "5517": {"comment":"Next pixel line"},
        "5518": {"comment":"All 10 lines?"},
        "5522": {"synopsis":"Opens a cell at column B of row C for an inserted character: the cells from B to the row's\nend move one cell right (the last character is dropped; the caller moved it on already).\nThen, with Z on entry, the cell at B is cleared; with NZ it receives the last character of\nthe row above, which is how the editor's insert ($1241, called row by row from the last one\nback) carries a character across rows.\nEntry: B = the column, C = the row, HL = the row's length byte, F = Z or NZ as above.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL.","comment":"Keep Z: blank the cell at the end"},
        "5523": {"comment":"The row's length"},
        "5524": {"comment":"...without the continuation bit"},
        "5526": {"comment":"Characters from the cursor to the end"},
        "5527": {"comment":"None to move?"},
        "5530": {"comment":"Bytes per character as a shift count"},
        "5536": {"synopsis":"Turns the character count into a byte count: one shift per step of $0B73.","comment":"Characters to bytes"},
        "5541": {"synopsis":"Moves the cells from the cursor to the row's end one cell right, from the last byte\nbackwards."},
        "5543": {"comment":"B = the row's length"},
        "5544": {"comment":"The last column"},
        "5546": {"comment":"HL = the row's last cell"},
        "5549": {"comment":"Last byte of the cell before it"},
        "5550": {"comment":"C = bytes to move per line"},
        "5554": {"comment":"The cell width"},
        "5559": {"comment":"...on: the last cell's last byte"},
        "5561": {"comment":"10 pixel lines"},
        "5563": {"synopsis":"Moves one pixel line of the row's tail one cell right, then steps 64 bytes on."},
        "5568": {"comment":"Move one pixel line one cell right"},
        "5570": {"comment":"Next pixel line, 64 bytes on"},
        "5578": {"comment":"All 10 lines?"},
        "5582": {"synopsis":"Fills the opened cell: cleared with Z on entry, otherwise from the last character of the row\nabove.","comment":"Blank the cell or pull a character in?"},
        "5583": {"comment":"Blank the cell at column B"},
        "5587": {"comment":"HL = the cell at column B"},
        "5591": {"comment":"DE = that cell, HL = the length byte"},
        "5592": {"comment":"The row above"},
        "5593": {"comment":"...and its length byte"},
        "5595": {"comment":"Its last column"},
        "5597": {"comment":"HL = the row above's last cell"},
        "5600": {"comment":"Bring that character down"},
        "5603": {"synopsis":"Device 1's function list (the keyboard): a count byte (4), then the words of functions 0-3,\nreached as RST $30 codes $10-$13: $10 KBD_POLL, $11 KBD_READ_KEY, $12 KBD_IGNORE, $13\nKBD_STATUS."},
        "5612": {"synopsis":"Sets up the keyboard (device 1); the boot calls it through its list at BOOT_INIT_TABLE. The\nrepeat delay $0B65 becomes 30 polls and the repeat rate $0B67 3 polls, the lock state $0B66\nis cleared, and so are the row states $0B51-$0B64 and the key buffer and repeat state\n$0BE5-$0BEE.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL.","comment":"Repeat delay: 30 polls"},
        "5617": {"comment":"Repeat rate: every 3rd poll"},
        "5622": {"comment":"No lock"},
        "5626": {"comment":"Clear the row states $0B51-$0B64"},
        "5638": {"comment":"Clear the key buffer, $0BE5-$0BEE"},
        "5650": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $13: whether a key is waiting.\nExit: C = $FF when a key is waiting, 0 when not; A = 0.","comment":"$FF when a key is waiting"},
        "5654": {"comment":"No error"},
        "5656": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $11: waits for a key and takes it. The key comes from KBD_POLL, which the\ninterrupt runs every frame.\nExit: C = the key's code; A = 0, or A = $F5 (break) when the break flag $0B16 is set\n(CTRL+ESC).\nChanges: AF, C, HL."},
        "5659": {"synopsis":"Waits until KBD_POLL flags a key at $0BE5.","comment":"A key waiting? (set by the poll)"},
        "5663": {"comment":"C = the key's code"},
        "5667": {"comment":"Taken"},
        "5669": {"comment":"Break flag set?"},
        "5673": {"comment":"No: A = 0"},
        "5674": {"comment":"A = $F5: break"},
        "5676": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $12: does nothing (a RET, shared with KBD_READ_KEY's end)."},
        "5677": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $10: the keyboard poll, run from the frame interrupt (its INT_POLL_DEVS calls\nfunction 0 of each device).\n1. Reads the 10 key rows into $0B51-$0B5A (KBD_SCAN).\n2. With the lock key (row 6 bit 5) down, a modifier held with it is locked in $0B66 (CTRL as\n   1: letters only, SHIFT as 2, the high-map modifier at row 7 bit 0 as 8; none clears the\n   lock), once per press ($0BE7).\n3. Notes the modifier held now in $0BE8 (CTRL 4, SHIFT 2, high 8).\n4. Looks for a newly pressed key (KBD_NEW_KEY); one found is put in $0BE9, flagged at $0BE5\n   and starts the repeat delay.\n5. Otherwise, after CTRL+P, it scans again until another key comes (a pause, inside the\n   interrupt).\n6. Otherwise it counts the repeat delay $0BEA and rate $0BEB down and, when one expires and\n   the last key is still held, reports that key again.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL, IY, the alternate BC, DE, HL.","comment":"The lock state"},
        "5680": {"comment":"...CTRL, SHIFT and high locks only"},
        "5682": {"comment":"Keep only the lowest one"},
        "5689": {"comment":"Read the 10 rows; IY = $0B51"},
        "5692": {"comment":"Set while the lock key is handled"},
        "5695": {"comment":"Lock key (row 6 bit 5) down?"},
        "5701": {"comment":"Already handled in this press?"},
        "5704": {"comment":"Mark it handled"},
        "5705": {"comment":"CTRL locks as 1: letters only"},
        "5710": {"comment":"The new lock state"},
        "5713": {"comment":"Something locked: no key this poll"},
        "5714": {"synopsis":"The lock key is up (or locked nothing): clears $0BE7 and notes the modifier held now.","comment":"Clear $0BE7 (B = 0 after the scan)"},
        "5715": {"comment":"CTRL counts as 4 here"},
        "5720": {"comment":"The modifier held now"},
        "5723": {"synopsis":"Finds a newly pressed key and reports it.","comment":"Z: the rows were just read"},
        "5724": {"synopsis":"Finds a new key, reading the rows again first when NZ (used while paused).","comment":"Look for a newly pressed key"},
        "5727": {"comment":"E = $FF: none?"},
        "5730": {"comment":"Store its code"},
        "5734": {"comment":"Flag a key waiting"},
        "5739": {"comment":"Start the repeat delay"},
        "5746": {"synopsis":"No new key: keeps scanning while paused, otherwise runs the key repeat.","comment":"Paused by CTRL+P?"},
        "5748": {"comment":"Yes: scan again until a key comes"},
        "5750": {"comment":"Count the repeat delay down"},
        "5756": {"comment":"...and the rate counter at $0BEB"},
        "5760": {"comment":"Neither expired"},
        "5761": {"comment":"C = the repeating key's bit"},
        "5765": {"comment":"DE = its row's known-key byte"},
        "5769": {"comment":"Row 8? (Carry is set here)"},
        "5775": {"comment":"Yes"},
        "5777": {"comment":"Row 9?"},
        "5785": {"comment":"No: not a cursor row"},
        "5787": {"synopsis":"The repeating key is a cursor key of rows 8-9 (bits 1, 2, 5, 6): when another of them is\nheld too, that one is made new again, so the two directions repeat in turn.","comment":"Bits 1, 2, 5, 6: the cursor keys"},
        "5789": {"comment":"Is the key one of them?"},
        "5792": {"comment":"The other three"},
        "5793": {"comment":"One of them held too?"},
        "5794": {"comment":"Let that one repeat next"},
        "5796": {"synopsis":"Repeats the key while it is held; once it is up, forgets every known key and stops the rate\ncounter.","comment":"Is the key still held?"},
        "5798": {"comment":"Yes: repeat it"},
        "5800": {"comment":"Up: forget every known key"},
        "5803": {"comment":"10 rows"},
        "5805": {"synopsis":"Clears the known-key bytes $0B5B-$0B64."},
        "5809": {"comment":"Stop the rate counter"},
        "5813": {"synopsis":"Drops the bits in A from the known keys of the row, so that the new-key search finds them\nagain, sets the rate counter from $0B67 and reports the key again.","comment":"Drop the bits from the known keys"},
        "5816": {"comment":"Next repeat after the rate count"},
        "5822": {"comment":"Report the key again"},
        "5824": {"synopsis":"Counts the byte at HL down to 0.\nExit: Carry set only when it reached 0 now; NC when it was 0 already or is not yet 0.\nChanges: AF.","comment":"Already 0? (NC)"},
        "5827": {"comment":"Not yet 0? (NC)"},
        "5829": {"comment":"Reached 0 now"},
        "5831": {"synopsis":"Finds a newly pressed key and translates it. With NZ on entry it reads the rows first\n(KBD_SCAN). The modifier and lock keys are removed from the rows; then for each row from 9\ndown to 0 the known keys $0B5B-$0B64 drop the released ones, and the lowest key down but not\nknown is taken and made known. Its row byte's address goes to $0BEC and its bit to $0BEE\n(for the repeat), the map index is (7 - bit) x 10 + row, and KBD_MAP_LOOKUP gives its code\nwith the modifiers $0BE8 toggled by the lock $0B66. Unless $0B68 is $FF, CTRL+P (code $10)\nis then swallowed and sets the pause flag $0BE6, and the next key clears the pause and,\nunless it is CTRL+ESC, the break flag $0B16.\nExit: E = $FF when there is no new key (or it was swallowed), otherwise C = the key's code;\nHL = $0BE6.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL, the alternate DE, HL.","comment":"NZ: read the rows first"},
        "5834": {"comment":"Not keys: SHIFT,"},
        "5838": {"comment":"CTRL,"},
        "5842": {"comment":"the high-map modifier"},
        "5846": {"comment":"and the lock key"},
        "5853": {"comment":"E = 10 rows, D = 0 for the index"},
        "5857": {"comment":"Past the known keys $0B5B-$0B64"},
        "5860": {"comment":"Past the rows read $0B51-$0B5A"},
        "5863": {"synopsis":"Moves to the next row down and compares what is down with what is known.","comment":"Row below (9 down to 0)"},
        "5866": {"comment":"Row number"},
        "5867": {"comment":"All rows done: E = $FF, no key"},
        "5869": {"comment":"B = keys down in this row"},
        "5871": {"comment":"Known keys still down"},
        "5874": {"comment":"Forget the released ones"},
        "5875": {"comment":"Down but not known: new"},
        "5876": {"comment":"None in this row?"},
        "5878": {"comment":"The lowest new bit"},
        "5882": {"comment":"Make it known"},
        "5886": {"comment":"Remember the row for the repeat"},
        "5889": {"comment":"...and the bit"},
        "5892": {"comment":"Index = (7 - bit) x 10"},
        "5894": {"synopsis":"Steps the index back 10 for each bit below the new key's."},
        "5896": {"comment":"Bit found?"},
        "5901": {"comment":"...+ row"},
        "5902": {"comment":"DE = the map index"},
        "5903": {"comment":"The modifier held"},
        "5907": {"comment":"...toggled by the lock"},
        "5913": {"comment":"C = the key's code"},
        "5917": {"comment":"$0B68 = $FF: no pause handling?"},
        "5922": {"comment":"Paused before?"},
        "5927": {"comment":"Port 03h's copy"},
        "5932": {"comment":"...selecting row 7"},
        "5936": {"comment":"Read row 7 (0 = down)"},
        "5938": {"comment":"ESC and CTRL both down?"},
        "5940": {"comment":"Yes: keep the break flag"},
        "5942": {"comment":"Clear the break flag"},
        "5948": {"synopsis":"Not paused: CTRL+P (code $10) starts a pause.","comment":"CTRL+P: pause?"},
        "5952": {"synopsis":"Ends a pause (or there was none).","comment":"Not paused"},
        "5955": {"synopsis":"Starts a pause: $0BE6 = $FF and the key is reported as none.","comment":"Paused: $0BE6 = $FF"},
        "5956": {"comment":"No key to report"},
        "5959": {"synopsis":"Translates a key into its code. The map is KEYMAP_CTRL with CTRL (bit 2), KEYMAP_SHIFT with\nSHIFT (bit 1), KEYMAP_HIGH with the row 7 bit 0 modifier (bit 3), else KEYMAP_PLAIN; with\nthe CTRL lock (bit 0) a letter (a-z and $90-$98 in the plain map) takes the shifted map\nwithout SHIFT and the plain one with it. A map byte of $FF (CTRL+ESC) sets the break flag\n$0B16 and gives $1B.\nEntry: DE = the index, (7 - bit) x 10 + row; B = the modifier bits.\nExit: C = the code.\nChanges: AF, C, HL.","comment":"Return through KML_READ"},
        "5963": {"comment":"KEYMAP_CTRL"},
        "5966": {"comment":"CTRL held?"},
        "5969": {"comment":"CTRL locked (caps)?"},
        "5973": {"comment":"The plain map's code"},
        "5978": {"comment":"Below 'a'?"},
        "5982": {"comment":"A letter a-z?"},
        "5986": {"comment":"Below $90?"},
        "5990": {"comment":"An accented letter $90-$98?"},
        "5994": {"synopsis":"A letter under the CTRL lock: the shifted map without SHIFT, the plain map with it.","comment":"Letters: shifted"},
        "5997": {"comment":"...unless SHIFT is down"},
        "6000": {"comment":"SHIFT: the plain map"},
        "6004": {"synopsis":"Not a letter under the CTRL lock: picks the map from SHIFT and the high-map modifier.","comment":"KEYMAP_SHIFT"},
        "6007": {"comment":"SHIFT held?"},
        "6010": {"comment":"KEYMAP_HIGH"},
        "6013": {"comment":"The high-map modifier held?"},
        "6016": {"comment":"Drop the return address"},
        "6017": {"comment":"KEYMAP_PLAIN"},
        "6020": {"synopsis":"Reads the code from the chosen map and turns $FF into ESC with the break flag."},
        "6021": {"comment":"C = the code"},
        "6023": {"comment":"$FF: CTRL+ESC?"},
        "6026": {"comment":"Set the break flag"},
        "6029": {"comment":"Report it as ESC"},
        "6032": {"synopsis":"Says which modifier key is down, from the rows read at IY: CTRL (row 7 bit 4) returns A as\ngiven, SHIFT (row 6 bit 3) 2, the high-map modifier (row 7 bit 0) 8, in that order of\npriority.\nExit: A = that value with NZ, or A = 0 with Z when none is down.\nChanges: AF.","comment":"CTRL (row 7 bit 4)? A as given"},
        "6037": {"comment":"SHIFT gives 2"},
        "6039": {"comment":"SHIFT (row 6 bit 3)?"},
        "6044": {"comment":"Row 7 bit 0, the high-map modifier?"},
        "6048": {"comment":"It gives 8"},
        "6051": {"comment":"None: A = 0, Z"},
        "6053": {"synopsis":"Reads the 10 key rows: each row number goes out on port 03h (its high nibble kept from\n$0B11, the port's copy) and port 58h is read and inverted, so a 1 bit is a key down.\nExit: $0B51-$0B5A = rows 0-9; IY = $0B51; HL = $0B5B; B = 0.\nChanges: AF, BC, HL, IY.","comment":"Port 03h's copy"},
        "6056": {"comment":"Keep the high nibble; row 0"},
        "6062": {"comment":"IY = the rows read"},
        "6065": {"comment":"10 rows"},
        "6067": {"synopsis":"Selects one row, reads it and stores it inverted."},
        "6068": {"comment":"Select the row"},
        "6070": {"comment":"Read it (0 = down)"},
        "6072": {"comment":"1 = down"},
        "6074": {"comment":"Next row"},
        "6079": {"synopsis":"The plain key map, the first of four 80-byte maps that touch ($17BF, $180F, $185F, $18AF),\nread by KBD_MAP_LOOKUP. Each map is indexed by (7 - bit) x 10 + row: ten entries (rows 0-9)\nfor bit 7, then ten for bit 6, down to bit 0. This one gives the codes with no modifier:\nletters in lower case, digits, $0D Return (row 5 bit 4), $20 space (row 7 bit 5), $1B ESC\n(row 7 bit 3), $08 (row 5 bit 0), the cursor keys of row 8 as $05 (bit 1), $04 (bit 5), $18\n(bit 2), $13 (bit 6), accented letters $90-$98. The modifier and lock positions hold $2A and\nare never looked up."},
        "6159": {"synopsis":"The shifted key map (80 bytes, same layout as KEYMAP_PLAIN): taken with SHIFT, and for\nletters under the CTRL lock without SHIFT. Upper-case letters, the digit keys' symbols,\naccented capitals $80-$88; the keys of rows 8-9 give the same codes as unshifted."},
        "6239": {"synopsis":"The CTRL key map (80 bytes, same layout as KEYMAP_PLAIN): letters give $01-$1A, other keys\ntheir control or other codes. CTRL+ESC (row 7 bit 3) is $FF, which the lookup turns into ESC\nwith the break flag; CTRL+P is $10, the pause."},
        "6319": {"synopsis":"The fourth key map (80 bytes, same layout as KEYMAP_PLAIN), taken with the modifier at row 7\nbit 0: most keys give codes $A0-$DF; Return, space, ESC and the keys of rows 8-9 keep their\nplain codes. It ends the maps at $18FE."},
        "6399": {"synopsis":"Device 4's function list (the printer, from the code at PRN_CHAR): a count of 3, then the\nwords of functions 0-2. Reached from the device table at DEVICE_TABLE."},
        "6406": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $42: device 4 function 2, writes a block to the printer. Hands PRN_CHAR to\nBLOCK_OUT, which calls it for each byte.\nEntry: DE = the first byte, BC = the count.\nExit: A = 0, or the first error code PRN_CHAR returned; $FA when the block lies above the\ntop of RAM in $0B19."},
        "6412": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $41: device 4 function 1, sends one character to the printer.\n1. Returns $F5 at once when $0B16 is $FF (break requested).\n2. Waits until port 59h bit 7 is set (the printer is ready).\n3. With interrupts off, writes the character to port 01h and pulses port 06h bit 7 low then\n   high (the strobe), keeping the other bits from $0B13, the copy of port 06h.\nEntry: C = the character.\nExit: A = 0, or $F5 on a break.\nChanges: A.","comment":"$0B16 is $FF when a break is requested"},
        "6416": {"comment":"Break: $F5"},
        "6419": {"comment":"Printer ready (port 59h bit 7)?"},
        "6424": {"comment":"No interrupt between data and strobe"},
        "6425": {"comment":"The character to the printer port"},
        "6428": {"comment":"Port 06h's copy, strobe bit low"},
        "6435": {"comment":"Strobe high again"},
        "6440": {"comment":"Done: no error"},
        "6441": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $40: device 4 function 0 does nothing (it is PRN_CHAR's closing RET). The\nprinter is not one of the devices 0-3 that the interrupt ticks."},
        "6442": {"synopsis":"Device 3's function list (the sound generator, from the code at SND_START): a count of 4,\nthen the words of functions 0-3. Reached from the device table at DEVICE_TABLE."},
        "6451": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $30: device 3 function 0, the sound's tick. The interrupt calls function 0 of\ndevices 0-3 on every frame (INT_POLL_DEVS) unless the device's bit in $0B10 is set. While a\nsound plays ($0B14 = $FF) it counts the duration in $0BEF down; at zero, or at once when a\nbreak is requested ($0B16 = $FF), it falls into SND_STOP.\nExit: A = $F5 when the sound stopped.","comment":"Is a sound playing ($0B14 = $FF)?"},
        "6456": {"comment":"Break requested?"},
        "6462": {"comment":"One frame less of the duration"},
        "6470": {"synopsis":"Stops the sound: clears $0B14 (no sound playing) and the duration in $0BEF, sets bit 3 of\n$0B10 (no more ticks for device 3) and clears port 05h bits 5-4 (the tone and its\ninterrupt), keeping the copy in $0B12.\nEntry: A = 0.\nExit: A = $F5.","comment":"No sound playing now"},
        "6476": {"comment":"No more ticks for device 3"},
        "6484": {"comment":"Port 05h: tone and its interrupt off"},
        "6494": {"comment":"Tell the caller the sound stopped"},
        "6496": {"synopsis":"RST $30 codes $31 and $32: device 3 functions 1 and 2 do nothing. Also device 3's boot\nroutine (the word at $0559, called by the loop at $02EF)."},
        "6497": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $33: device 3 function 3, starts a sound.\n1. Unless $0B15 is $FF, waits until less than 2 ticks remain of the sound playing now.\n2. Clears $0B14; a break requested ($0B16 = $FF) or a duration of 0 stops the sound\n   (SND_STOP).\n3. Puts the duration in $0BEF, the volume in port 06h bits 5-2 (copy $0B13), the pitch's\n   high bits in port 05h bits 3-0 with bit 4 set (the tone on; copy $0B12) and its low byte\n   in port 04h.\n4. Sets $0B14 and $0B71 to $FF and clears bit 3 of $0B10, so that SND_TICK counts it down.\nEntry: B = the duration in frames (0 stops the sound), C bits 3-0 = the volume, D bits 3-0\nand E = the pitch (the sound divider's value).\nExit: A = 0.","comment":"$0B15 = $FF: start at once"},
        "6503": {"comment":"Fewer than 2 frames left of the old sound?"},
        "6510": {"synopsis":"The previous sound is over (or no waiting was asked for): sets up the new one."},
        "6511": {"comment":"No sound playing"},
        "6514": {"comment":"Break requested?"},
        "6520": {"comment":"Duration 0?"},
        "6524": {"comment":"Means: stop the sound"},
        "6529": {"synopsis":"A duration was given: programs the sound ports.","comment":"The duration in frames"},
        "6532": {"comment":"Volume to bits 5-2 of port 06h"},
        "6538": {"comment":"Keep port 06h's other bits"},
        "6549": {"comment":"Pitch high bits, bit 4: tone on"},
        "6555": {"comment":"Keep port 05h's bits 7-6"},
        "6566": {"comment":"Pitch low byte"},
        "6569": {"comment":"A sound is playing"},
        "6577": {"comment":"Let SND_TICK count it down"},
        "6587": {"synopsis":"Device 5's function list: a count of 6, then the words of functions 0-5. Functions 1-5 and\nthe boot routine run in EXTH (the extension ROM, $F3D8-$F3F1), which clears port 05h bits\n7-6 at boot (the tape motor in Klive's core): the cassette. Observed: SAVE calls its\nfunctions 0-4 over and over."},
        "6600": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $53: device 5 function 3, runs in EXTH at $F3E2 (CAS_TO_EXTH).","comment":"EXTH's entry for function 3"},
        "6605": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $54: device 5 function 4, runs in EXTH at $F3E7 (CAS_TO_EXTH). RUN calls it\nthrough the SYS high chip's $FC3E.","comment":"EXTH's entry for function 4"},
        "6610": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $51: device 5 function 1 (the character function, as for devices 1 and 4), runs\nin EXTH at $F3D8 (CAS_TO_EXTH).","comment":"EXTH's entry for function 1"},
        "6615": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $52: device 5 function 2 (the block function, as for device 4), runs in EXTH at\n$F3DD (CAS_TO_EXTH).","comment":"EXTH's entry for function 2"},
        "6620": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $55: device 5 function 5, runs in EXTH at $F3EC (CAS_TO_EXTH).","comment":"EXTH's entry for function 5"},
        "6623": {"synopsis":"Runs device 5's code in EXTH: the SYS high chip's $FFF0 writes $F0 to port 02h (and its copy\nat $0003), which puts EXT in page 3, and jumps to HL. Each EXTH entry calls its routine and\ncomes back through EXTH's own $FFF0 (port 02h = $70, SYS in page 3) to CAS_NOP, which\nreturns to the caller.\nEntry: HL = the EXTH entry ($F3D8-$F3F1).","comment":"EXT into page 3, jump to HL"},
        "6626": {"synopsis":"Device 5's boot routine (the word at $055B, called by the loop at $02EF): EXTH's $F3F1 calls\n$F707, which clears RAM $0BF0-$0E34, sets $0B6C to $80 and turns port 05h bits 7-6 off.","comment":"EXTH's boot entry"},
        "6631": {"synopsis":"RST $30 code $50: device 5 function 0 does nothing. Also where every EXTH entry of\nCAS_TO_EXTH comes back to, with SYS in page 3 again."},
        "6632": {"synopsis":"\"(c) ISL\": seven bytes of text that no code reads (the run did not touch them), between the\ndevice code and BASIC's start. ISL is the Intelligent Software Ltd of the copyright text at\nCOPYRIGHT_TEXT."},
        "6639": {"synopsis":"BASIC's start, reached from the reset code's JP at $0331 once SYS is in page 3. With $0B21\nnonzero it skips to BASIC_WARM; otherwise:\n1. Clears BASIC's variables at $1700-$19EE (IX = $1700 points at them from now on) and puts\n   the program's start, $19EF, in $1720 and $1722.\n2. Copies the restart code from the SYS high chip's $FB5B to RAM $0008-$002E (RST $08 raises\n   the error whose code follows it, RST $10 raises the error in A unless Z is set).\n3. Clears the program (CLEAR_PROG), sets the palette from BOOT_PALETTE and the border to 2.\n4. Draws the title: seven rows of two \"VIDEOTON\"s closing in, a third one, then \"TV\n   COMPUTER\" letter by letter in random colours, over and over until a key is pressed.\n5. Reads the key, sets the screen up again (device 0 function 4, C = 1) and prints the\n   banner and the free memory, then falls into BASIC_WARM.","comment":"IX = $1700, BASIC's variables"},
        "6645": {"comment":"$0B21 nonzero: keep the program"},
        "6652": {"comment":"Bytes to clear: $1700-$19EE"},
        "6655": {"synopsis":"Clears $1700-$19EE, one byte a turn, until BC runs out.","comment":"A = 0"},
        "6656": {"comment":"Next byte, count down"},
        "6661": {"comment":"The program starts at BASIC_START"},
        "6667": {"comment":"The restart code from the SYS high chip"},
        "6670": {"comment":"To RAM $0008 (RST $08 to $0028)"},
        "6676": {"comment":"Copy it"},
        "6678": {"comment":"No program"},
        "6681": {"comment":"BOOT_PALETTE"},
        "6684": {"comment":"Device 0 function C: the palette"},
        "6685": {"comment":"Code $0C"},
        "6686": {"comment":"Border colour 2"},
        "6691": {"comment":"Output to channel 0 (device 0)"},
        "6695": {"comment":"First row and column"},
        "6698": {"comment":"12 spaces between the first pair"},
        "6700": {"synopsis":"One row of the title: \"VIDEOTON\", A spaces, \"VIDEOTON\"; the next row starts one lower and\ntwo to the right with two spaces fewer."},
        "6702": {"comment":"Device 0 function 3: B, C to the cursor"},
        "6703": {"comment":"Code $03"},
        "6704": {"comment":"The left \"VIDEOTON\""},
        "6709": {"comment":"A spaces"},
        "6712": {"comment":"The right \"VIDEOTON\""},
        "6715": {"comment":"New line"},
        "6720": {"comment":"Next row: one lower, two to the right"},
        "6723": {"comment":"Two spaces fewer: all rows done?"},
        "6727": {"comment":"Row and column of the last \"VIDEOTON\""},
        "6730": {"comment":"Cursor to B, C"},
        "6731": {"comment":"Code $03"},
        "6735": {"synopsis":"Prints \"TV  COMPUTER\" at row/column $0B12, each letter in a random colour 1-3, and repeats\nuntil a key is waiting.","comment":"Row and column of \"TV  COMPUTER\""},
        "6738": {"comment":"Cursor to B, C"},
        "6739": {"comment":"Code $03"},
        "6740": {"comment":"TV_COMPUTER_TXT"},
        "6743": {"comment":"B = 12 letters"},
        "6744": {"synopsis":"One letter of \"TV  COMPUTER\", then a random nonzero colour (the SYS high chip's $E6D8, the\nrandom number generator) for the next one and a short delay."},
        "6745": {"comment":"The letter"},
        "6746": {"comment":"Print it"},
        "6751": {"synopsis":"Draws random numbers until the bottom two bits are not both zero.","comment":"A random number"},
        "6754": {"comment":"Colour 0?"},
        "6760": {"comment":"The ink colour for the next letter"},
        "6763": {"synopsis":"A short delay: counts C down to zero."},
        "6766": {"comment":"Next letter"},
        "6768": {"comment":"Device in $0B01, function 3: a key waiting?"},
        "6769": {"comment":"Code $93"},
        "6770": {"comment":"C = $FF when one is"},
        "6773": {"comment":"Device in $0B01, function 1: read the key"},
        "6774": {"comment":"Code $91"},
        "6775": {"comment":"C = 1"},
        "6777": {"comment":"Device 0 function 4: the screen set up"},
        "6778": {"comment":"Code $04"},
        "6779": {"comment":"Border from A"},
        "6782": {"comment":"Normal ink"},
        "6785": {"comment":"Print the banner that follows"},
        "6788": {"synopsis":"The banner, a length-prefixed string printed by the SYS high chip's $FE79: \"TV COMPUTER\nBASIC 1.2\", \"Copyright 1985 VIDEOTON\" and an empty line."},
        "6839": {"comment":"Free memory as a number"},
        "6842": {"comment":"Print it"},
        "6845": {"comment":"Drop it from the IY stack"},
        "6848": {"comment":"Print \" bytes free\""},
        "6851": {"synopsis":"\" bytes free\" and an empty line, a length-prefixed string printed by the SYS high chip's\n$FE79 after the free memory count."},
        "6867": {"synopsis":"BASIC's warm start, also the end of BASIC_START: clears $0B21 and the program's run state\n(RESET_RUN), then falls into COMMAND_LOOP.","comment":"No warm start requested any more"},
        "6871": {"comment":"Clear the run state"},
        "6874": {"synopsis":"The command loop, where BASIC returns when a program ends, after NEW, after DELETE and after\nan error:\n1. Resets the stack to $16AC; clears the program if $1703 was set.\n2. Sends output to channel $20 (device 2) and clears bit 2 of $1700 (no program running).\n3. Prints \"ok\" unless bit 1 of $1700 says an error message was just printed (the SYS high\n   chip's $FDB8 sets it), then falls into OK_CMD.","comment":"The stack's base"},
        "6877": {"comment":"$1703 set: the program must go"},
        "6884": {"comment":"Then clear it"},
        "6887": {"comment":"Output to channel $20 (device 2)"},
        "6891": {"comment":"No program running"},
        "6895": {"comment":"Error message just printed?"},
        "6901": {"synopsis":"Prints \"ok\" in the normal ink, then a new line (through PROMPT_NL).","comment":"Normal ink"},
        "6904": {"comment":"Print \"ok\""},
        "6907": {"synopsis":"\"ok\" and $0B, a length-prefixed string printed by the SYS high chip's $FE79: BASIC's prompt."},
        "6911": {"synopsis":"Clears the error-printed flag (bit 1 of $1700) and starts a new line.","comment":"Error-printed flag off"},
        "6915": {"comment":"New line"},
        "6918": {"synopsis":"The OK statement (token $E4), and the line-entry loop that COMMAND_LOOP falls into:\n1. Reads a line from the keyboard (the SYS high chip's $FF4F); on a break ($F5) goes to the\n   STOP code at $FFA3, on $EC prompts again.\n2. Tokenises it into $1735 (TOKENISE) and reads a number from its start onto the IY stack\n   (the SYS high chip's $F914).\n3. An empty line is dropped. A line number (1-4 digits before the point, so 1-9999;\n   LINE_NUMBER checks) makes it a program line: it is stored with STORE_LINE (a number alone\n   deletes that line). Anything else runs at once from RUN_DIRECT.","comment":"Read a line from the keyboard"},
        "6921": {"comment":"Break?"},
        "6926": {"comment":"Code $EC: prompt again"},
        "6930": {"comment":"Tokenise it into $1735"},
        "6933": {"comment":"DE = the tokenised line"},
        "6935": {"comment":"Past its length byte"},
        "6936": {"comment":"Read a number onto the IY stack"},
        "6939": {"comment":"Carry: the reader took at least one digit"},
        "6941": {"comment":"Token $A8 or the end of the line?"},
        "6946": {"comment":"Not $FF: run the line"},
        "6949": {"synopsis":"Nothing to do: drops the number from the IY stack and reads the next line.","comment":"Drop the number, next line"},
        "6954": {"synopsis":"Checks whether the number is a line number: with an exponent byte (IY+8) of $40-$43 and a\nnonzero (IY+6).","comment":"Bit 7: the exponent overflowed, so no line number"},
        "6957": {"comment":"B = the byte after the number"},
        "6958": {"comment":"The number's exponent byte"},
        "6961": {"comment":"Fewer than 1 digit?"},
        "6965": {"comment":"More than 4 digits?"},
        "6969": {"comment":"A zero line number?"},
        "6975": {"synopsis":"Skips the spaces after the line number.","comment":"Skip the spaces after the number"},
        "6982": {"comment":"HL = the line's length byte"},
        "6983": {"comment":"The tokenised length"},
        "6985": {"comment":"+2 for the line number"},
        "6987": {"comment":"Less what the number took"},
        "6990": {"comment":"C = the stored line's length"},
        "6991": {"comment":"DE = the line number"},
        "6994": {"comment":"HL = the text"},
        "6995": {"comment":"Line number high, low, length in front"},
        "7001": {"comment":"The current line"},
        "7004": {"comment":"Put it in the program"},
        "7007": {"comment":"Next line from the keyboard"},
        "7009": {"synopsis":"Runs the tokenised line at once: puts a header in front of it (length + 2, line number 0),\nmakes it the current line in $170C and falls into the statement loop.","comment":"HL = the tokenised line"},
        "7010": {"comment":"Drop the number"},
        "7013": {"comment":"C = the tokenised length"},
        "7015": {"comment":"Line number 0 in front"},
        "7019": {"comment":"The length with the line number"},
        "7022": {"synopsis":"Starts the line at HL: makes it the current line ($170C), prints its number when TRACE is on\n(bit 0 of $1700) and steps HL past the header to the first statement.","comment":"The current line"},
        "7025": {"comment":"To the line number"},
        "7026": {"comment":"TRACE on?"},
        "7030": {"comment":"Print \"<line>\""},
        "7033": {"comment":"Bit 0 of $1702 off"},
        "7037": {"comment":"Past the line number"},
        "7039": {"comment":"Text pointer to HL'"},
        "7040": {"synopsis":"The ':' statement (token $FD), and the statement loop: every statement starts here.\n1. Checks for a break (the SYS high chip's $FF9D) and saves IY, the expression stack, in\n   $171A.\n2. Skips spaces; at the end of the line ($FF) or a '!' comment ($FE) goes to NEXT_LINE.\n3. A byte below $CA is not a statement keyword: the SYS high chip's $E3BC takes the line (an\n   assignment without LET; observed: A=5 reaches $E3BC, LET B=5 does not).\n4. Otherwise it jumps to the token's word in the table at STMT_TABLE (the word at STMT_TABLE\n   + 2 x (token XOR $FF)), fetching the next token first unless the token is DATA, REM, ':'\n   or '!', with the stack reset to $16AC. Statement routines run with the program text\n   pointer in HL' and the current token in B'.","comment":"Text pointer to HL"},
        "7041": {"synopsis":"The statement loop, entered with the text pointer in HL (CONTINUE, DATA and the SYS high\nchip jump here).","comment":"Break requested?"},
        "7044": {"comment":"Save the expression stack"},
        "7048": {"synopsis":"Skips the spaces before the statement.","comment":"The next byte"},
        "7049": {"comment":"End of line or '!'?"},
        "7054": {"comment":"Space?"},
        "7058": {"comment":"Not a statement keyword?"},
        "7063": {"comment":"Save the token"},
        "7064": {"comment":"Text pointer back to HL'"},
        "7065": {"comment":"Token XOR $FF"},
        "7066": {"comment":"x 2 + STMT_TABLE: its word in the table"},
        "7072": {"comment":"HL = the statement routine"},
        "7077": {"comment":"Not DATA, REM, ':' or '!'?"},
        "7079": {"comment":"Then fetch the next token"},
        "7082": {"comment":"The stack's base"},
        "7085": {"comment":"Run the statement"},
        "7086": {"synopsis":"The end of a statement, after fetching the next token (the SYS high chip's $FC43): falls\ninto STMT_END.","comment":"Fetch the next token"},
        "7089": {"synopsis":"The end of a statement: a ':' starts the next one (COLON_CMD), the end of the line goes to\nNEXT_LINE, anything else is error 1 (\"Not understood\", the SYS high chip's $FD5A).","comment":"A = the current token"},
        "7092": {"comment":"':'?"},
        "7096": {"comment":"Not the end of the line: error 1"},
        "7099": {"synopsis":"The REM and '!' statements (tokens $FC and $FE), and the end of every line: steps from the\ncurrent line ($170C) by its length byte to the next. A line follows: runs it (RUN_LINE). The\nprogram has ended: drops the FOR/GOSUB entries that point into the direct-command line, then\ngoes to the END statement ($E10E) when a program was running, to COMMAND_LOOP when not.","comment":"The current line"},
        "7102": {"comment":"Its length"},
        "7105": {"comment":"To the next line"},
        "7106": {"synopsis":"Is there a line at HL (its length byte nonzero)? RUN enters here with the program's first\nline.","comment":"Is there one?"},
        "7109": {"synopsis":"Drops the IY stack's entries of type $2B and $06 (GOSUB and FOR; the type byte is the\nentry's length. Observed after FOR and GOSUB: a 6-byte entry of type $06 above a 43-byte\nentry of type $2B) whose address word (IY+3) is not above $1831, the direct-command line.","comment":"The IY stack's top entry type"},
        "7112": {"comment":"Type $2B?"},
        "7116": {"comment":"Type $06?"},
        "7120": {"synopsis":"No more entries to drop: to the END statement when a program was running (bit 2 of $1700),\nelse to COMMAND_LOOP.","comment":"Was a program running?"},
        "7124": {"comment":"No: back to the command loop"},
        "7127": {"comment":"Yes: the END statement"},
        "7130": {"synopsis":"An entry of type $2B or $06: dropped when its address points into the direct-command line.","comment":"The entry's address word"},
        "7136": {"comment":"Above the direct-command line?"},
        "7141": {"comment":"Then keep it"},
        "7143": {"comment":"The type is the entry's length"},
        "7146": {"comment":"Drop the entry"},
        "7148": {"comment":"The new stack top"},
        "7154": {"synopsis":"Prints \"VIDEOTON\", the length-prefixed string that follows the call to the SYS high chip's\n$FE79. Used by the title.","comment":"Print the string that follows"},
        "7157": {"synopsis":"\"VIDEOTON\", the length-prefixed string printed by PRINT_VIDEOTON."},
        "7167": {"synopsis":"\"TV  COMPUTER\", a length-prefixed string (12 letters) printed letter by letter by\nTITLE_LETTER."},
        "7180": {"synopsis":"Prints A spaces (the SYS high chip's $FEC7 prints one).\nEntry: A = the count (0 prints none).\nChanges: A, F.","comment":"All printed?"},
        "7183": {"comment":"Print a space"},
        "7189": {"synopsis":"The palette at start: four bytes for ports 60h-63h, written by device 0 function C\n(GFX_SET_PALETTE), which BASIC_START calls with DE pointing here."},
        "7193": {"synopsis":"Turns a typed line into tokens.\n1. Copies the text at HL to $1736 on, putting each keyword's token (from TOKEN_TABLE, $FE\n   down to $95) in place of its letters; letters outside quotes are made capitals by the SYS\n   high chip's $FBBF.\n2. Text in quotes is copied as typed; so is the rest of the line after REM or '!', and\n   DATA's text up to the next ':'.\n3. The tokens $A6, $A5 and $A3 ('=>', '><', '=<') are stored as $9E, $9D and $9B ('>=',\n   '<>', '<=').\n4. Ends the line with $FF and puts its length in $1735.\nEntry: HL = the text.\nExit: HL = $1735, the tokenised line.\nChanges: A, BC, DE.","comment":"Keep IY"},
        "7195": {"comment":"DE = the text"},
        "7196": {"comment":"Output from $1736"},
        "7200": {"comment":"Not in quotes"},
        "7203": {"synopsis":"No keyword here: keeps the character just copied and moves on.","comment":"Next character"},
        "7204": {"synopsis":"Starts a new item: from the top of the token table, at token $FE.","comment":"TOKEN_TABLE"},
        "7207": {"comment":"The first token"},
        "7210": {"synopsis":"Copies the next character to the output, remembering where it came from in $171C; stops at\nthe end of the text ($FF).","comment":"Next output byte"},
        "7212": {"comment":"Where the item starts"},
        "7216": {"comment":"Next character, capitals"},
        "7219": {"comment":"Copy it"},
        "7222": {"comment":"End of the text?"},
        "7225": {"comment":"Copying everything (REM)?"},
        "7229": {"comment":"Or DATA (B = ':')?"},
        "7233": {"synopsis":"A '\"' starts quoted text: B holds the quote, C the previous state.","comment":"A '\"'?"},
        "7238": {"comment":"Save the state, B = the quote"},
        "7243": {"synopsis":"Matches the text against the table's current word, one character a turn.","comment":"Next character"},
        "7247": {"comment":"End of the text?"},
        "7250": {"comment":"The closing quote?"},
        "7253": {"comment":"Back to the saved state"},
        "7256": {"comment":"A '\"' ends quoted text"},
        "7260": {"comment":"DATA ends at ':': store a ':' token"},
        "7264": {"synopsis":"Inside quotes, REM or DATA: copy the character. Outside: compare it with the table's next\nletter (bit 7 marks the last).","comment":"Copying?"},
        "7268": {"comment":"Same as the table's letter?"},
        "7272": {"comment":"Next table letter"},
        "7273": {"comment":"Not its last: go on matching"},
        "7275": {"comment":"A = the token"},
        "7277": {"comment":"'=>'?"},
        "7281": {"comment":"'><'?"},
        "7285": {"comment":"'=<'?"},
        "7289": {"synopsis":"'=>', '><' or '=<': stored as '>=', '<>' or '<='.","comment":"To '>=', '<>' or '<='"},
        "7291": {"synopsis":"A keyword matched: DATA, REM and '!' switch to copying (DATA up to the next ':').","comment":"':'?"},
        "7295": {"comment":"Below DATA?"},
        "7299": {"comment":"REM or '!': copy the rest"},
        "7302": {"comment":"DATA: copy up to ':'"},
        "7304": {"synopsis":"Stores the token in place of the keyword's letters.","comment":"Store the token"},
        "7309": {"synopsis":"No match: back to where the item started, past the rest of the table's word to the next\ntoken; at the table's end the character is kept as it is.","comment":"Back to the item's start"},
        "7313": {"synopsis":"Skips to the byte after the one with bit 7 set.","comment":"The word's last letter?"},
        "7318": {"comment":"Next token"},
        "7321": {"comment":"The table's end ($FF)?"},
        "7327": {"synopsis":"The end of the text: puts $FF after the line and its length in $1735.","comment":"After the last byte"},
        "7331": {"comment":"The end marker $FF"},
        "7332": {"comment":"Length = end - $1735"},
        "7339": {"comment":"Put it in $1735"},
        "7343": {"synopsis":"Puts a line into the program: deletes the line with the same number, if any, and inserts the\nnew one in order unless it is empty (the first text byte $FF), then resets the run state.\nEntry: HL = the line (length, line number, text), DE = its line number, B = its first text\nbyte.","comment":"Keep the new line"},
        "7347": {"comment":"Is the number already there?"},
        "7350": {"comment":"Then delete the old line"},
        "7354": {"comment":"An empty line ($FF)?"},
        "7358": {"comment":"Room for the new line"},
        "7361": {"comment":"DE = the gap"},
        "7365": {"comment":"Copy the line in"},
        "7369": {"synopsis":"Makes room for BC bytes in front of the line at HL: resets the run state, checks the memory\n(the SYS high chip's $FC8E) and moves the rest of the program up.\nEntry: HL = where, BC = how many.\nExit: HL = the gap.","comment":"Reset the run state"},
        "7372": {"comment":"Enough memory for BC bytes?"},
        "7377": {"comment":"HL = the program's end"},
        "7382": {"comment":"Bytes to move"},
        "7389": {"comment":"Destination: end + BC"},
        "7392": {"comment":"With the end marker"},
        "7393": {"comment":"Move up"},
        "7395": {"comment":"HL = the gap"},
        "7398": {"synopsis":"Deletes the line at HL by moving the rest of the program, up to its end marker, down over\nit.\nEntry: HL = the line.","comment":"The line's length"},
        "7402": {"comment":"The next line"},
        "7404": {"comment":"HL = the program's end"},
        "7409": {"comment":"Bytes to move"},
        "7416": {"comment":"With the end marker"},
        "7417": {"comment":"Move down"},
        "7420": {"synopsis":"Resets the state that depends on the program: the expression stack (IY = the top of RAM in\n$0B19, emptied), $170E (no CONTINUE), $1714, $1724 = $F094, the random seed at $1709-$170B,\nthe DATA pointer $1712 (the program's start) and $1726 (just past the program's end).\nChanges: IY."},
        "7423": {"comment":"The expression stack's base"},
        "7427": {"comment":"Empty it"},
        "7433": {"comment":"No CONTINUE"},
        "7439": {"comment":"$1724 = $F094"},
        "7445": {"comment":"Random seed: $1709 = 9"},
        "7449": {"comment":"$170A = $1709"},
        "7452": {"comment":"DATA from the program's start"},
        "7458": {"comment":"The program's end"},
        "7461": {"comment":"Past it: $1726"},
        "7469": {"synopsis":"Prints a program line: its number (the SYS high chip's $FF19), then its text with the tokens\nspelt out ($FEDD), then a new line when carry is set ($FE8E, which sends $0B first when Z is\nset). LIST and the error report ($FDC0) use it.\nEntry: HL = the line, carry = end with a new line, Z = $0B before it."},
        "7470": {"comment":"DE = the line number"},
        "7477": {"comment":"Print it"},
        "7481": {"comment":"Print the text, tokens spelt out"},
        "7485": {"comment":"No new line?"},
        "7489": {"synopsis":"Finds the end of the program: FIND_LINE for line $FFFF.","comment":"Line $FFFF: past every line"},
        "7492": {"synopsis":"FIND_LINE for the line number in HL."},
        "7493": {"synopsis":"Finds a line in the program, which is in line-number order from $1722.\nEntry: DE = the line number.\nExit: Z and no carry: found, HL = the line. Carry: not there, HL = the first line after it,\nor the end marker (a zero length; Z too).\nChanges: A, BC.","comment":"The program's start"},
        "7499": {"synopsis":"Next line: is this the end marker, or the line wanted?"},
        "7500": {"comment":"The line's length"},
        "7503": {"comment":"Length 0: the end, carry"},
        "7506": {"comment":"DE = this line's number"},
        "7513": {"comment":"Compare with the one wanted"},
        "7519": {"comment":"Found"},
        "7520": {"comment":"Below it: go on"},
        "7523": {"synopsis":"Error $0A, \"Cannot CONTINUE\" (RST $08 with the code after it).","comment":"Error $0A: Cannot CONTINUE"},
        "7524": {"synopsis":"The error code for the RST $08 before it: $0A, \"Cannot \" and the CONTINUE token in the SYS\nhigh chip's message table at $FDC6."},
        "7525": {"synopsis":"The CONTINUE statement (token $F8). Inside a running program it does nothing. Otherwise it\nmarks a program running and goes on from the line in $170E with the text pointer in $1710\n(the place a break left); with no such line it is error $0A, \"Cannot CONTINUE\".","comment":"Program running?"},
        "7529": {"comment":"Then do nothing"},
        "7532": {"comment":"A program runs from now"},
        "7536": {"comment":"The line to go on from"},
        "7539": {"comment":"None?"},
        "7543": {"comment":"The current line"},
        "7546": {"comment":"The text pointer to go on from"},
        "7552": {"synopsis":"The LLIST statement (token $E9): LIST_CMD to channel $40 (device 4, the printer).","comment":"Channel $40: the printer"},
        "7554": {"comment":"NZ: LLIST"},
        "7557": {"synopsis":"The LIST statement (token $EA): to channel $20 (device 2).\n1. Takes the channel (the SYS high chip's $FBEE, which may take another).\n2. Takes ranges separated by ',' (token $A4): a line, from-to with '-' (token $A2), either\n   end missing, or nothing for the whole program.\n3. Prints each line of each range with PRINT_LINE, checking for a break between lines.\n   DELETE enters at $1D93, the operand of the LD D,$F6 at $1D92: OR $37 clears carry, the\n   flag in AF' that makes the loop delete the lines (DELETE_LINE) instead of printing them.","comment":"Channel $20"},
        "7559": {"comment":"Z: LIST"},
        "7560": {"synopsis":"Selects the output channel in C."},
        "7561": {"comment":"Take the channel"},
        "7567": {"comment":"Channel $20 still? Z: $0B before new lines"},
        "7570": {"comment":"DELETE enters at $1D93: OR $37, NC"},
        "7572": {"synopsis":"LIST and LLIST: carry set, the flag in AF' that makes the loop print the lines; Z is kept\ntoo, and PRINT_LINE sends a $0B before each new line when it is set (channel $20).","comment":"Carry: print the lines"},
        "7573": {"comment":"The flag to AF'"},
        "7574": {"comment":"The current token"},
        "7577": {"comment":"From line 0"},
        "7580": {"comment":"To line $FFFF"},
        "7583": {"comment":"':' or the end: whole program"},
        "7589": {"synopsis":"A range is done: a ',' starts the next one; otherwise LIST ends the statement and DELETE\ngoes back to COMMAND_LOOP.","comment":"The current token"},
        "7592": {"comment":"','?"},
        "7596": {"comment":"LIST or DELETE?"},
        "7597": {"comment":"LIST: end the statement"},
        "7600": {"comment":"DELETE: back to the command loop"},
        "7603": {"synopsis":"Past the ',': the next range.","comment":"Past the ','"},
        "7606": {"synopsis":"Reads a range: an optional first line, an optional '-' and an optional last line.","comment":"The first line number"},
        "7609": {"comment":"Given?"},
        "7612": {"comment":"Else it must be '-'"},
        "7617": {"comment":"From line 0"},
        "7620": {"synopsis":"DE = the first line (0 when missing)."},
        "7621": {"comment":"Keep the first line"},
        "7622": {"comment":"A '-'?"},
        "7626": {"comment":"Past the '-'"},
        "7629": {"comment":"The last line number"},
        "7632": {"synopsis":"Finds the first line of the range.","comment":"HL = the first line number"},
        "7633": {"synopsis":"From the line in HL to the line in DE.","comment":"Keep the last"},
        "7634": {"comment":"Find the first line"},
        "7638": {"synopsis":"One line: past the program's end or the range's last line ends the range.","comment":"The program's end?"},
        "7643": {"comment":"BC = this line's number"},
        "7649": {"comment":"Past the last one wanted?"},
        "7655": {"comment":"Keep the last line number"},
        "7656": {"comment":"LIST or DELETE?"},
        "7659": {"comment":"LIST: print the line"},
        "7664": {"synopsis":"DELETE: removes the line instead of printing it.","comment":"DELETE: remove it"},
        "7668": {"synopsis":"Checks for a break and goes on with the next line."},
        "7669": {"comment":"Break requested?"},
        "7675": {"synopsis":"Reads an optional line number: when the current token is 2 (a number follows), the number in\nDE (the SYS high chip's $FAC3) and the next token.\nExit: DE = the number, or $FFFF when there is none.","comment":"No number"},
        "7678": {"comment":"Token 2: a number follows?"},
        "7681": {"comment":"The number"},
        "7685": {"comment":"And the next token"},
        "7688": {"synopsis":"The NEW statement (token $E6): turns TRACE off, clears the program and goes back to\nCOMMAND_LOOP.","comment":"Back to the command loop after"},
        "7692": {"comment":"TRACE off"},
        "7696": {"synopsis":"Clears the program: its start in $1722 from $1720, an end marker (0) there, then RESET_RUN.","comment":"Where programs start"},
        "7702": {"comment":"No lines"},
        "7707": {"synopsis":"The RUN statement (token $D8): from the line given (the SYS high chip's $FBDE; error 2,\n\"Line missing\", when it is not there) or the program's first line. Resets the run state,\ncalls device 5 function 4 and the RAM table's entry 5 function 4 (the SYS high chip's\n$FC3E), marks a program running (bit 2 of $1700) and starts the line at NEXT_LINE_2.","comment":"The program's first line"},
        "7710": {"comment":"A line number given?"},
        "7712": {"comment":"Then find that line"},
        "7715": {"comment":"Reset the run state"},
        "7718": {"comment":"Device 5 function 4 and more"},
        "7721": {"comment":"A program runs"},
        "7725": {"comment":"Start the line at HL"},
        "7729": {"synopsis":"The TRACE statement (token $D3): TRACE ON or TRACE OFF, with an optional channel (the SYS\nhigh chip's $FBEC, $20 by default) kept in $1706. Sets or clears bit 0 of $1700; anything\nelse is error 1.","comment":"The channel, $20 by default"},
        "7732": {"comment":"The TRACE channel"},
        "7735": {"comment":"ON?"},
        "7739": {"comment":"OFF?"},
        "7744": {"comment":"TRACE off"},
        "7750": {"synopsis":"TRACE ON: bit 0 of $1700 set.","comment":"TRACE on"},
        "7754": {"synopsis":"Ends the statement."},
        "7757": {"synopsis":"Prints the current line's number as \"<n>\" to the TRACE channel ($1706 into $1705, the output\nchannel) when a program is running. Called by RUN_LINE when TRACE is on.\nEntry: HL = the line number's address.","comment":"Program running?"},
        "7762": {"comment":"Output to the TRACE channel"},
        "7769": {"comment":"HL = the line number"},
        "7773": {"comment":"'<'"},
        "7778": {"comment":"Print the number"},
        "7784": {"comment":"'>'"},
        "7789": {"synopsis":"BASIC's keywords, one word per token from $FE ('!') down to $95 (')'), each ended by bit 7\non its last letter, between two $FF bytes. Six one-byte words '!' stand for tokens with no\nkeyword ($CF-$CA, $AD-$A9). TOKENISE searches it from $1E6E; the SYS high chip's $FEDD\nspells a token out by counting words from $1E6D (the $FF counts as one), which LIST and the\nerror messages use."},
        "8149": {"synopsis":"Words for the error messages, ended by bit 7 like the keywords: \" missing\", \"rgument\", \"Bad\n\", \"No \", \"Cannot \". They follow TOKEN_TABLE, so the SYS high chip's $FEDD spells them as\ntokens $93-$8F (the $FF at $1FD4 is $94). Its message table at $FDC6 builds messages from\nthem, e.g. \"A\" $92 $93 = \"Argument missing\", $8F with the CONTINUE token = \"Cannot\nCONTINUE\"."},
        "8178": {"synopsis":"The DATA statement (token $FB): skips the text up to the next ':' or the end of the line and\ngoes back to the statement loop (STMT_LOOP).","comment":"Text pointer to HL"},
        "8179": {"synopsis":"Skips bytes until a ':' ($FD) or the end of the line."},
        "8180": {"comment":"':' or the end of the line?"},
        "8188": {"synopsis":"The CLS statement (token $F9): device 0 function 5 (GFX_CLS) clears the graphics screen, RST\n$10 raises its error if it returned one, then ends the statement through the JP at $1FFF to\nSTMT_END.","comment":"Device 0 function 5"},
        "8189": {"comment":"Code $05"},
        "8190": {"comment":"Error unless A was 0"},
        "8191": {"synopsis":"JP STMT_END: the opcode C3 is this chip's last byte and its operand is the SYS high chip's\nfirst two bytes (B1 DB), so CLS_CMD ends here and goes on to STMT_END."}
      }
    }
  },
